\par\par \b \uc2  Early Tokyo -2: \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fear Supports USD/JPY's Floor \b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 1999\par 03:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  "I think the dollar/yen might drop further, but bids chiefly from short-term speculative accounts such as some U.S. funds might support the dollar floor around Y121.00," Kazuki Fujita, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at the Industrial Bank of Japan said. \par \par \uc2  But some traders noted that while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  had repeatedly said that "premature" strengthening of the yen is unwelcome for Japan's economy, the MOF might not take action in the immediate future with remarks Monday from Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa that the MOF did not take extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "While there certainly is a fear of another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the finance ministry might not act as the dollar/yen hovers above the 121.00 level," Takeo Okabe, deputy-manager of Market Trading Division at Daiwa Bank, said. \par \par \uc2  Okabe added, "Miyazawa said (Monday) the MOF did not take extreme actions on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In addition, the U.S. kept saying \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation is not appropriate. Under the circumstances, to my mind, it is rather difficult for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action now." \par \par \uc2  In the aftermath of a drop to Y123.27 through a key support at Y123.50, the euro was expected to drop further, many \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Tokyo said. \par \par \uc2  "The euro/yen has been top-heavy. While market fear of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might somewhat support the euro, the euro will gradually fall below Y123.00," the IBJ's Fujita said. \par \par \uc2  Market attention was also fixed on the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the BOJ previously supported the euro/yen when the euro fell to the Y123.50 level. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda repeated Wednesday morning that premature yen strengthening is not desirable, adding that the government might take decisive action. The dollar gained slightly to Y121.42 and euro to Y123.57. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen gained slightly after Kuroda's remarks this morning, but the dollar's sentiment is seen still weak. I expect the dollar might gradually lower in the Asian day," Takao Yoshida, manager of the market trading division at Daiwa Bank said. \par \par \uc2  The euro is trading at Y123.48, slightly up from Y123.45 in New York late yesterday. The euro was quoted late in Tokyo yesterday at Y123.78. \par \par \uc2  Sterling is at $1.5578, up from $1.5570 in New York late yesterday. Sterling was at $1.5570 in Tokyo late yesterday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is seen trading between Y120.95-Y121.80, while the euro is expected to trade between $1.0120-$1.0180 for the rest of the Asian day. \par \par \uc2  -By Hidenobu Kageyama; 813-5255-2929; hkageyama@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 14-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0105GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7e057nb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
