\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro/dollar adjourns parity challenge. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,243 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 1999\par 17:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 14 (Reuters) - The euro on Wednesday enjoyed a respite against the dollar, rising over a cent from life lows, but was still seen at risk of hitting parity with the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  Only 24 hours ago, traders were betting the euro would have hit $1.0 by now, but the euro defied all predictions and rallied above $1.02, from Tuesday's life low of $1.0104. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said, however, that the euro was still suffering from a major confidence crisis linked to lack of coordination among the euro zone officials which hampered its outlook. \par \par \uc2  "As things stand now, a fall below parity has to be the short-term risk," said Derek Halpenny at BoT-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  By 1516 GMT, the euro stood at the day's peak of $1.0218/25, compared with $1.0193/98 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "We will get to parity, but not necessarily in the next week," said Jeffrey Woodruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  The belaguered European unit had dipped to $1.0126 earlier in the session as Horst Siebert, a senior economic adviser to the German government, said that the euro could fall to $0.90. \par \par \uc2  The euro had later staged a comeback as Siebert clarified his views to Reuters Television, saying that $0.90 was not a serious forecast. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also benefited from market caution about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was fuelled by scattered talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/dollar fell out of bed on the Siebert comments, then he retracted them a bit and the euro bounced higher," said a senior trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the euro had recovered to 123.05/15 yen from falling earlier in the day to within a whisker of its life low at 122.53 set on June 18, the day the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros for yen on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  "People are obviously keeping half an eye out for the Bank of Japan," said a European bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Further weakness in euro/yen was likely to encourage \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the twin threat of ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the euro and BOJ action to curb yen strength was providing support to the beleaguered European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Halpenny said, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of the BOJ would not be purely dictated by euro/yen levels, as the BOJ would rather monitor the stability of 120 yen for dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 120.39/44 yen, its trough since the BOJ sold yen for dollars on July 5, against 121.26/31 late in European on Tuesday and below a low of 120.50 set June 30. \par \par \uc2  The collapse of significant psychological levels on the two major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs would probably raise U.S. authorities' eyebrows. \par \par \uc2  "A break of 120 yen coupled with euro/dollar at parity would be a concern for also for the U.S.," said Halpenny. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. would then be tempted to voice some criticism asking for policy to be sorted out in Europe." \par \par \uc2  A batch of benign U.S. economic data did not help the dollar to regain much ground against the bouyant yen. \par \par \uc2  U.S. June producer prices fell 0.1 percent, the first dip since a 0.5 percent drop in February, pointing to a low inflationary environment that could allow the Fed to hike rates if wanted. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the market took little notice of the data because most had their minds fixed on the bubbling problems in Latin America, which could harm U.S. stocks and thus the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Worries that Argentina would default on its debt had weighed on the dollar on Tuesday and the dollar recovered only after Argentina's ruling party's presidential candidate Eduardo Duhalde denied calling for the country's debt to be pardoned. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  By 1516 GMT, the euro stood at the day's peak of $1.0218/25, compared with $1.0193/98 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "We will get to parity, but not necessarily in the next week," said Jeffrey Woodruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  The belaguered European unit had dipped to $1.0126 earlier in the session as Horst Siebert, a senior economic adviser to the German government, said that the euro could fall to $0.90. \par \par \uc2  The euro had later staged a comeback as Siebert clarified his views to Reuters Television, saying that $0.90 was not a serious forecast. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also benefited from market caution about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was fuelled by scattered talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/dollar fell out of bed on the Siebert comments, then he retracted them a bit and the euro bounced higher," said a senior trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the euro had recovered to 123.05/15 yen from fal