\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-MOF's Kuroda sees euro recovering soon. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 1999\par 10:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 14 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said on Wednesday that the euro is underestimated and that he thinks it will recover soon. \par \par \uc2  Asked about a recent fall in the euro and his view on the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (ECB) policy on the euro, Kuroda said: "I don't intend to request anything to the ECB. In my personal opinion, the euro is underestimated. It will, for sure, recover soon." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, who succeeded Eisuke Sakakibara as vice finance minister for international affairs last week, added that cooperation among Group of Seven (G7) nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained solid. \par \par \uc2  "Authorities (of the G7 nations) are keeping in close contact on every appropriate occasion, and that has not changed." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda reiterated that he was not seeking specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, adding that he hoped \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements would reflect economic fundamentals without causing excessive movements. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers understands \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Kuroda refrained from directly answering and said: "The G7 framework remains unchanged." \par \par \uc2  He stressed that Japan's policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained the same after he replaced Sakakibara, adding that the ministry would take decisive action to prevent premature strength in the yen. "It is still unclear if the Japanese economy has turned upward despite some positive signs. Under this circumstance, volatile movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not favourable," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Premature strength in the yen is undesirable for the Japanese and global economies. We will take decisive action if necessary to avoid such movements," he added. \par \par \uc2  Asked his view on the possible impact on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economies of yen weakness resulting from yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kuroda said: "We don't intend to orchestrate a weaker yen." \par \par \uc2  He added that he did not think a weaker yen would have a negative impact on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Regarding movements in Japan's long-term interest rates amid talks for further fiscal spending to get the nation's flagging economy out of prolonged recession, Kuroda said discussion on such further spending should start after data is known on economic growth in the April-June quarter. \par \par \uc2  If Japanese government bonds are issued when fund demand is tight, the issuance would have a negative impact on long-term interest rates, Kuroda said. But he added that if the economy recovers and fund demand becomes strong, there will be no need for Japan to issue more government bonds. \par \par \uc2  "Although the ministry, as an issuer of Japanese government bonds, will monitor the movements in long-term interest rates carefully, I don't expect a rapid rise in the rates," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also said: \par \par \uc2  * Japan has only a small amount of gold reserves and there is no need to rush in selling its gold reserves despite a recent fall in gold prices. He declined to comment on specific levels of gold prices, but added that he was not particularly worried about weakness in gold prices. \par \par \uc2  * The ministry will continue to promote the yen's globalisation, adding that he hoped Japanese firms operating in Asia would start using the yen more, rather than the dollar. Although the MOF does not seek the exclusive use of the yen in Asia, it hopes to see the yen used as much as the dollar in Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  ((Yoko Nishikawa, Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7e04m20\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
