\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Jittery euro eyes lows vs dollar, yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 1999\par 10:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 14 (Reuters) - The euro fell to within a third of a cent of life lows against the dollar and close to life lows against the yen in Europe on Wednesday after a senior economic adviser to the German government said the euro could fall as low as $0.90. \par \par \uc2  The euro dropped nearly half a cent against the dollar in Europe after the comment by Horst Siebert, a member of the German government's official economic advisory council called the Five Wise Men. \par \par \uc2  "People are in the mood to listen to this sort of thing," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  Siebert said the euro's fall would become dangerous if it boosted domestic inflation and led to a loss of public confidence in the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Siebert's comments had a disproportionate impact on the market because the euro is now so widely expected to reach parity and because the comments were made in thin markets, as Tokyo dealing was drawing to a close and London dealing had barely started. \par \par \uc2  The euro's losses against the yen once more raised the spectre of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at 122.64/68 at 0737 GMT, against a low of 122.53 reached on June 18, when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros for yen on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro was trading at $1.0126/31, from Tuesday's life low of $1.0104 and from $1.0193/98 in late European trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said on Wednesday the euro was underestimated and would recover soon. He also reiterated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not seeking specific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was trading at 121.10/15 at 0742 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The dollar earlier hit a low of 120.95 yen, its weakest since the Bank of Japan bought dollars for yen on July 5. \par \par \uc2  Yen strength was fuelled as the Nikkei closed at its highest level for 21 months. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  preferred to avoid obvious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  "They have clearly tried to make a point of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they are not expected to," said Breedon. "The next round is likely to be in euro/yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  Lingering concern about Argentina following debt default rumours earlier this week was also weighing on dollar/yen, analysts said. But these fears were waning after Latin American markets bounced on Tuesday, they added. \par \par \uc2  "There is some apprehension about U.S. producer prices around this morning, but if they are fairly benign, it will encourage the U.S. stock market," said Ravi Bulchandani, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  U.S. June producer price and retail sales data are due at 1230 GMT. Producer prices are forecast to have risen by 0.1 percent since May, and retail sales are seen up 0.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average dipped 0.23 percent on Tuesday amid worries over Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell to within half a cent of Friday's near-three-year lows against the dollar, tracking euro/dollar losses. \par \par \uc2  British June employment data and May wages data are due at 0830 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The pound was trading at $1.5550/55 at 0815 GMT, from a low of $1.5515 and from $1.5578/88 in late European trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7e04l9i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
