\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro off low in pre-parity reprieve. \b0\par\par\uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 1999\par 13:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 13 (Reuters) - The euro staged a mini-rebound on Tuesday after slipping within just over a cent of parity against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  It was squeezed higher by dealers short of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after they pushed it to lifetime lows on two consecutive days. \par \par \uc2  Despite a slightly better performance, the question was when, not if, the euro will trade at one-to-one against the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "I suspect the market has got a bit short of euros, so we're getting a little squeeze (higher)," said Neal Kimberley, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. "It was due for a bit of a bounce, but in a week or so we'll see parity." \par \par \uc2  The euro has now lost over 15 percent against the dollar since it started trading in January, amid pessimism about the euro zone's economy in contrast to brisk, non-inflationary growth in the United States. This month alone it has shed 2.7 percent, leaving some big players short, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There isn't as much volume as one would have expected on touching new lows," said Frank Mikulecz, managing director at Baxter Financial Services in Dublin. \par \par \uc2  Mikulecz said players who had tried to pick a bottom in euro/dollar were staying sidelined now. "They don't dare to go short at these levels. But we will see $1.00 sooner or later." \par \par \uc2  Moreover, trading was subdued as dealers were waiting for a news briefing by Group of Seven finance ministers after their meeting in Brussels on the reconstruction of war-ravaged Kosovo. \par \par \uc2  At 1017 GMT the euro was at $1.0145/47, up from $1.0134/36 late Monday and from its $1.0108 low. Dolllar losses against the yen also helped the euro. Dollar/yen was at 121.54/60 versus 122.16/26. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar fell as automated sell orders were triggered, first at 121.90 and then larger ones at 121.80. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was dragged down in line, falling to a 3-1/2 week low at 123.23 before recovering to 123.37/50, down from 123.75/95 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro stuttered higher after U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers urged euro-zone countries to boost their economies. Summers, arriving for the G7 meeting, said the euro/dollar rate may be discussed but would not be a focus. \par \par \uc2  The "primary issue" for Europe was to concentrate on sound economic policies to stimulate domestic demand-led growth in their flagging economies, Summers said. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. is getting a bit impatient that European leaders are burying their heads in the sand and not detecting that there is a problem in Europe," said Stephen Lewis, chief economist at Monument Derivatives. \par \par \uc2  The second day of poor German data behind the euro's drop pushed the euro to new lows in late Tokyo trade. German retail sales fell a real 2.8 percent after a 4.2 percent drop in April. \par \par \uc2  Further data pain might loom on Thursday when Italy is slated to release its industrial production data for May. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was a sizeable bid around $1.0105, said to be protecting an option strike level at $1.0100. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's drop below 122 yen, where the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, and the euro's fall below 123.50 yen, also a level that has attrated BOJ attention, made traders wary of renewed action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he was not thinking about any specific \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday upgraded its assessment of the economy, but policymakers, still cautious on the outlook, said it must continue taking steps to nurture its nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Planning Agency said in its July monthly report the economy had been "somewhat improving as the effects of various policies have permeated." It was the second consecutive month that the EPA was more upbeat in its assessment. ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792)). \par \par \uc2  The euro has now weakened over 15 percent against the dollar since it started trading in January, amid pessimism about the euro zone's economy in contrast to brisk, non-inflationary growth in the United States. This month alone it has shed 2.7 percent, leaving some big players short, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There isn't as much volume as one would have expected on touching new lows," said Frank Mikulecz, managing director at Baxter Financial Services in Dublin. \par \par \uc2  Mikulecz said players who had tried to pick a bottom in euro/dollar were staying sidelined now. "They don't dare to go short at these levels. But we will see $1.00 sooner or later." \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he was not thinking about any specific \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday upgraded its assessment of the economy, but policymakers, still cautious on the outlook, said it must continue taking steps to nurture its nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Planning Agency said in its July monthly report the economy had been "somewhat improving as the effects of various policies have permeated". It was the second consecutive month that the EPA was more upbeat in its assessment. ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 6284)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7d04rvv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
