\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro hits new lows against dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 1999\par 17:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 12 (Reuters) - The euro sank to lifetime lows against the dollar, which also climbed to eight-year peaks versus the Swiss franc, on Monday at the start of a data-packed week expected to confirm solid, non-inflationary U.S. growth. \par \par \uc2  The latest lurch lower in the euro/dollar rate came as European Union finance ministers met in Brussels, with euro weakness and greater economic policy coordination among the 11 euro zone countries high on the agenda. \par \par \uc2  News of an unexpected 0.2 percent drop in German industrial output in May sent the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a low of $1.0114, 15 percent below the highs set hours after its debut on January 4. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the data confirmed the picture of a weak euro zone economy, while expectations of brisk growth in the U.S. continues to fuel investors' appetite for dollars. \par \par \uc2  "The German industrial production figures were disappointing and seem to indicate that German recovery is faltering," said Stuart Green, economist at Credit Lyonnaise in London. \par \par \uc2  "If we see some benign inflation data out of the U.S. at the end of this week the euro could fall to parity," he added. \par \par \uc2  Remarks by French Finance Minister Dominique Strauss-Kahn  that Italy and Germany's budget situation had improved in recent weeks and that Italy's fiscal outlook was now "markedly more reassuring" provided limited relief to the ailing euro. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they are focusing on the reaction of U.S. asset markets to producer price and retail sales data on Wednesday, consumer price on Thursday and industrial production on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Further dollar gains could be expected if the figures support the rosy outlook of the U.S. economy, which would also be positive for the stock market. The Dow Jones industrial average rose to a record closing high of 11,193.70 last Friday. \par \par \uc2  By 1430 GMT the euro was at $1.0135/37, less than 1/4 cent from its life lows and compared with $1.0188/95 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The market's passion for dollars against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also reflected in a surge to an eight-year high at 1.5892 Swiss francs. Chartists say the dollar is gearing up to attack a long-standing barrier at 1.5937 francs which would lift the U.S. unit to its peaks in nearly 10 years against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  "Market participants remain focused on growth differentials between the U.S. and Europe," said American Express Bank's senior technical analyst Gerry Celaya. \par \par \uc2  "This is putting further pressure on the Swiss franc and could be enough to put risk levels from 1.5935 to 1.60 out of play and allows for a drive towards the 1989 high near 1.80 over the coming months," he added. The dollar stood at 1.5846/56 towards the end of the European session on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the first wave of euro selling was spurred early in Europe as Finnish Finance Minister Sauli Niinisto said there were "quite natural reasons" for the weak euro, linked to the solid U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  His comments came as Finland chaired the first EU finance ministers meeting of its six-month presidency. They echoed remarks last week by German Finance Minister Hans Eichel and EU Commission President Romano Prodi who also said they had no problem with the weak euro. \par \par \uc2  An EU monetary source said the euro zone countries were aiming for a deal to restrict comment on the euro to the head of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to the president of the 11-nation club, a move that analysts said would help the euro. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 20 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts last Thursday found the euro has a two thirds chance of sinking to dollar parity. The analysts expected the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if it actually hits one-to-one or below. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was stuck at 122 yen amid wariness that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will repeat its dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yen from strengthening. Dollar/yen was at 122.22/31, down a touch from 122.33/38 yen in late Europe on Friday. London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6284)) \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7c04qfz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
