\par\par \b \uc2  = \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Dollar Seen Extending Gains On U.S. Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marianne Sullivan \par \uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 1999\par 00:00\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Positive U.S. economic data and increasing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows into U.S. equities should be enough to free the dollar from the range in which it has languished against the yen for the last few weeks. \par \par \uc2  While the U.S. dollar has displayed its strength clearly against many European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its upside against the yen has been capped by the strong investor demand for Japanese equities, according to analysts. \par \par \uc2  This week, however, the U.S. consumer price index and retail sales are expected to underline continued strong U.S. growth and low inflation, the best combination for a strong dollar, said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital in New York. \par \par \uc2  That data, strong or not, isn't supposed to offer any fresh clues to Federal Reserve watchers trying to pinpoint future moves in U.S. interest rates. The market is still pricing in a 50% chance that the Fed will raise interest rates at its meeting Aug. 24 and should maintain this mixed stance well into August, said Willmore. \par \par \uc2  But with interest-rate differentials already on its side, the dollar shouldn't have much trouble pushing the euro to a fresh all-time low as the embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  grinds ever closer to parity. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro bottomed out last Thursday at $1.0146 before rebounding above\par $1.0200. Late in Friday in New York, the euro was trading at $1.0180,\par down from $1.0220. The dollar was trading at Y122.38, down from Y122.58\par late Thursday. Sterling was worth $1.5502, down from $1.5594.\par          Widespread Selling Of Euros\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Michael McGuinness, head of North American sales at American Express\par in New York, said euro selling is widespread among corporates and funds\par as markets lose all confidence in European policy makers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Last week, the euro's inability to get a boost from the first positive\par signs of euro-zone growth in a long while suggests the market's worry is\par not as much with Europe's sagging economy as it is with those who frame\par its economic policy, some analysts maintain.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The euro is so cheap now that it would make sense to see some buying,\par especially since the market is so short the euro, but you never know\par which European official will put his foot in his mouth next," Barclays'\par Willmore said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the euro's 13% depreciation since January, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials continue to downplay their concern with its level and\par seem to have sent the market the message they have no plans to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The attitude, say many traders, has only served to feed the\par euro selling frenzy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Marc Chandler, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Chicago's Mellon Bank, said last\par week's slew of euro lows brought out the "capitulation trades."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We saw a lot of corporates and money managers who could no longer\par take the pain and bailed out," Chandler said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Unlike its European counterpart, the Bank of Japan seems to have\par succeeded in convincing yen enthusiasts it is serious about preventing\par what it calls the yen's premature appreciation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After several rounds of yen-supportive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the last of which\par came July 5, the market players have suppressed their yen-buying urge.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With little stamina left to push the dollar lower, the market is thus\par expected to push the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  above last week's high of Y122.74,\par helped not only by strong U.S. data but also by what analysts say is\par some waning interest in Japan's Nikkei 225 stock index, which has lured\par investors with its astounding gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If, however, the Nikkei does reach a new high and closes above 18,000\par as yen-buying interest pushes the dollar toward Y121.60 and the euro to\par Y123.75, conditions will be ripe for another BOJ action, McGuinness\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe's central bankers have been willing participants in the BOJ's\par moves that have helped support the euro. But a sharp rise in the yen\par during the U.S. day could pose a problem for Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which would have to call on the U.S. Treasury and its newly\par confirmed secretary, Lawrence Summers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Summers, perhaps in an effort to stop any suitors in their tracks,\par clearly stated last week, in his first television interview since taking\par over the Treasury post, that Japan should focus on strengthening its\par economic fundamentals through demand-led growth. Market forces, he said,\par should be allowed to prevail instead of seeking to manipulate\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par His view came as no surprise. Even in late June during his\par confirmation hearing before the Senate Banking Committee, Summers\par underlined his commitment to the strong dollar policy of his\par predecessor. And well aware that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had begun its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  two\par weeks earlier, Summers stated "no nation can devalue its way to\par prosperity."\par -By Marianne Sullivan; 201-938-4385\par email:marianne.sullivan@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  07-11-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1800EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv7b016x8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
