\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Wary euro keeps off lifelow vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 1999\par 17:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 9 (Reuters) - The euro managed to hold above recent lifetime lows against a firmer dollar in Europe on Friday, helped by a flurry of buying in the afternoon session. \par \par \uc2  Aggressive euro/yen buying triggered a rally of more than 1/2 a cent in euro/dollar which lifted the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back above $1.02 and sparked wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But trading quickly cooled down and traders said the steepness of the move had been amplified by the thinness and nervousness of the market. \par \par \uc2  "There was a big buying order on euro/yen which spilled into euro/dollar," said a European bank dealer in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "This led to a bit of panic in the market as people are still nervous about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Dealers added that the euro was temporarily assisted higher as the market did not want to be caught overly short of euro/yen before the weekend for fear the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, as it did this week. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0187/93 by 1455 GMT, up 0.5 percent from its Thursday life trough of $1.0144 but off an early high of $1.0242. The euro was at $1.0201/05 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile euro/yen was at 124.71/81 from 124.92/24 yen in late trading on Thursday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that the rally in euro/yen started as the euro approached 124.50 yen. This is the upper boundary of the 123.50 to 124.50-yen band which many dealers and analysts think is a red-alert area for the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japan's new top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, pledged overnight that he would maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of his predecessor, Eisuke Sakakibara, and reiterated he was ready to act if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements became excessive. \par \par \uc2  His remarks kept the market on its guard and ensured the dollar remained trapped in a tight trading range. Dollar/yen was at 122.34/39 from 122.55/60 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is caught by the need to sell dollars, to buy yen and the fears that the Bank of Japan is going to come in and run them over," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read. "As such, the appetite to play in that market is negligible right now." \par \par \uc2  As dollar/yen remained sidelined, dealers expected activity to concentrated in the euro over the next few days and said that its outlook against the dollar remained fragile. \par \par \uc2  Jeff Woodruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Boston said that as long that the outlook for U.S. interest rates remained firm, the U.S. dollar was bound to retain its vigor. \par \par \uc2  Less then two cents separate the euro from parity with the dollar. The risk of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen rising as this level drew closer but traders said policymakers had yet to show they were concerned by the euro's drop. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody is worried about parity with the dollar, but does it really make a difference for Europe?" said the European bank trader. "At the same time, at the end of the day it is the U.S. who are paying the bill and they cannot be interested in a fall which widens their trade deficit," he added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar set a new 33-month high against the pound and approached eight-year highs versus the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell to $1.5507, its lowest since September 1996, and was mired at $1.5531/36 at 1539 GMT compared with $1.5584/94 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "In a period of generalised dollar strength, the pound seems to be clinging more closely to the euro than to the dollar. That is the primary reason for sterling's recent fall," said Adam Cole, UK economist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar came just shy of Thursday's eight-year highs of 1.5789 against the Swiss franc. Dollar/Swiss was last at 1.5765/70 from 1.5727/36 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "This led to a bit of panic in the market as people are still nervous about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Dealers added that the euro was temporarily assisted higher as the market did not want to be caught overly short of euro/yen before the weekend for fear the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, as it did this week. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0187/93 by 1455 GMT, up 0.5 percent from its Thursday life trough of $1.0144 but off an early high of $1.0242. The euro was at $1.0201/05 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile euro/yen was at 124.71/81 from 124.92/24 yen in late trading on Thursday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that the rally in euro/yen started as the euro approached 124.50 yen. This is the upper boundary of the 123.50 to 124.50-yen band which many dealers and analysts think is a red-alert area for the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japan's new top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, pledged overnight that he would maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of his predecessor, Eisuke Sakakibara, and reiterated he was ready to act if \b \highl