\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Kuroda vows to stay course as 'Mr Yen' exits. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 1999\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 9 (Reuters) - Haruhiko Kuroda publicly donned the mantle of Japan's top financial diplomat on Friday, vowing to carry on the policies of his flamboyant predecessor, Eisuke Sakakibara, especially on curbing the strong yen. A news conference to mark Sakakibara's retirement and replacement by Kuroda as vice minister for international affairs displayed the oft-noted contrast between the brash Sakakibara, dubbed "Mr Yen" for his sway over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and the scholarly, understated Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  But Kuroda, who had succeeded Sakakibara as the ministry's International Bureau chief in a previous bureaucratic personnel reshuffle two years ago, stressed that although the performers had changed, the song would remain the same. \par \par \uc2  "As I myself have worked under bureau director-general and vice minister Sakakibara, our thinking on policy is absolutely the same and I want to continue with his policies," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  His priorities would continue to be achieving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability and a strong Japanese economic recovery, pressing on with aid to crisis-hit Asian economies and reforming the international financial system. \par \par \uc2  "As we have often said, a premature strengthening of the yen is unfavourable for the Japanese and world economies," Kuroda said. "There is no change in this stance." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  five times in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since releasing bullish January-March growth data on June 10, selling yen for dollars and even euros to prevent a muscular Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from choking off a nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  Saying bright signs had emerged but that the Japanese economy remained in a delicate state, Kuroda said he was ready to send signals to the market, or if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were excessive, to "take action" again. \par \par \uc2  The ministry had no range for dollar/yen trading in mind, but wanted to ensure that exchange rates reflected fundamentals and moved in a stable manner, he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, playing down perceptions that Kuroda lacks his commanding presence in the markets, said with his characteristic cackle: "Although he looks like a nice guy on the outside, I think in fact he will be tougher as vice minister than I." \par \par \uc2  The exchange market shrugged off the remarks by Sakakibara and Kuroda, having heard the yen-curbing remarks daily in recent weeks as reporters ambushed both men on their way to the office. \par \par \uc2  The sole point of market interest was their reaction to a chastisement of their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy by new U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  "I think the focus in Japan has to be on strengthening the fundamentals, which go back to domestic demand-led growth (and) to allowing market forces to operate...not seeking to manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Summers said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the approach that leads to long-term prosperity," he said in his first interview after replacing his departing boss, Robert Rubin. \par \par \uc2  Typically, Kuroda sidestepped the issue, saying only that Group of Seven cooperation for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability remains firm. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara insisted that in his four years as bureau chief and vice minister, "We have not once manipulated the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  Although \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stock and bond prices are ultimately set by the market, he said, "the market is not perfect" and the authorities must take a "tough position" in expressing their message to the markets. \par \par \uc2  "I think Mr Summers absolutely does not think that we have been manipulating prices," declared Sakakibara, who was briefly on the Harvard economics faculty with Summers in the early 1980s. \par \par \uc2  On his own future, Japan's best-known bureaucrat said he planned to consider his options - such as teaching jobs in Tokyo or Australia - while fulfilling his new position as part-time adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "New Vice Minister Kuroda will take centre stage to do his work now," Sakakibara said. "I will be on the sidelines or supporting from behind the scenes. I have exited the stage." ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7904ur8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
