\par\par \b \uc2  Euro finds support but struggles, dlr/yen boxed in. \b0\par\par\uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 1999\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 9 (Reuters) - The euro found support against the dollar and the yen by late Tokyo trade on Friday as many players squared short positions ahead of the weekend due to wariness over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese and European central banks. \par \par \uc2  Nervousness that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/dollar and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/yen made many operators cautious about holding short positions over the weekend after the euro rebounded from a lifetime low of $1.0146 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen rates were trapped in a tight range throughout the day, with the downside limited by the possibility of dollar bids from the BOJ while the upside was capped by offers from exporters and expectations of options-linked sales. \par \par \uc2  Activity was thin throughout the day in dollar/yen trade, but many speculators were looking for incentives to bid for dollars. \par \par \uc2  A much-awaited news conference in the late afternoon by Japan's new Vice Finance Minister of International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda and his predecessor Eisuke Sakakibara failed to draw dollar buying against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda pledged on Friday to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of Sakakibara, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to block a premature yen rise. \par \par \uc2  At 0715 GMT, the dollar was at 122.50/60 yen against 122.55/65 in late New York trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said:"We are not thinking of a specific range (for dollar/yen rates). But in order to curb excessive market movements, we will engage in a dialogue with the market and, as needed, take action. We don't have any specific figures in mind." \par \par \uc2  Although there was some interest among speculators in driving the dollar higher against the yen, other operators were nervous about buying the greenback aggressively, eyeing buy orders below 122 yen and sell orders above 122.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Speculators were somewhat inclined to buy dollars. They went a bit long before the news conference, but an absence of buying incentives forced some liquidation," a city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were bids by overseas operators below 122 yen, thought to be aimed at hedging positions in Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's upside was expected to be limited by continued aggressive selling interest by Japanese exporters around 123 yen and options-related offers lurking around 123.20. \par \par \uc2  "Although sales by exporters seem to be slowing down a bit, the market was still cautious about buying the dollar heavily," a Japanese trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Also speculation is growing that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be able to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily beyond 124 or 125 yen, especially after comments by (U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence) Summers." \par \par \uc2  Summers commented in an interview with CNBC on Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "manipulation" was not the way for Japan to achieve economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, who has publicly charged the U.S. with having an inconsistent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy over the years, told Friday's news conference that the Group of Seven's united stance on the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability remained firm. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara added that he was sure Summers did not mean by his comments that he thought \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices in its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0747 GMT, the euro stood at $1.0209/14 against $1.0220/25 in late New York on Thursday. Despite finding support earlier in the session, it lacked the energy to break resistance at $1.0250. \par \par \uc2  Since its launch on January 4 the euro has fallen about 15 percent against the dollar, dragged down by the glaring contrast between a sagging euro-zone economy and its robust U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Hans Eichel and European Commission President Romano Prodi both said on Thursday they saw no problem with the current low level of the euro. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7904ts1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
