\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Dollar eyes upside vs European  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  in US. \b0\par\par\uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 1999\par 16:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In NEW YORK story headlined "Dollar eyes upside vs European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in US", please read in sixth paragraph ... to prop up the battered euro ... instead of ... to prop up the battered yen ... (changing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to euro from yen). A corrected version follows: \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 9 (Reuters) - The dollar was stronger against the euro, British pound and Swiss franc as U.S. trade opened on Friday, in the greenback's latest show of strength against its European counterparts. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit started softer against the yen, although it staged a short rally shortly after the open. The euro also briefly pushed sharply higher against the yen, sparking rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It certainly looks like something is going on, but I'm not sure what," said a trader at a large Japanese bank. "There are rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but at this point it's just talk." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was thinly traded and very jittery, especially with the euro headed on a steady downward course toward parity with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The market is very wary of more Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tone down yen strength against both the dollar and euro, after the Japanese authorities entered the market five times in recent weeks. The morning's upturn in euro/yen occurred as the pair dipped toward the 124.50 level -- an area traders had earlier feared could trigger BoJ action. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of European central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the battered euro is also keeping traders on high alert. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen opened at 122.10/20, down from 122.55/65 at the previous close. It perked up by around a quarter of a yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk, before drifting back slightly. \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, opened at $1.0201/03, down from $1.0220/25 at the previous U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's been strong all week across the board, so it wouldn't be a huge stretch to see the euro come up higher later in the day," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a large U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "Given lack of news or fundamentals or technicals you have to think the market's going to trend on short-term factors," \par \par \uc2  But although the euro was holding its head above Thursday's fresh record low of $1.0144, the market is looking for more declines towards parity with the dollar in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "We're not at all far away. Next week? Why not," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also firmer against the British pound, having extended recent gains to new 33-month highs against sterling in European and early U.S. trade. It opened at $1.5510/20, against $1.5580/90 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Traders say the $1.55 level is well defended, but a dip below $1.5485 will take cable to its lowest since August 1996. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also opened stronger against the Swiss franc, which has been sliding steadily in tandem with the euro. Dollar/Swiss opened at 1.5742/52 francs, against 1.5703/13 at the previous close, but it refrained from making fresh eight-year highs for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The commodity-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little changed against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened unchanged at Canadian $1.4702/12. The Australian dollar traded at $0.6665/70, unchanged from the previous day's close. \par \par \uc2  ((--N.A. Treasury Desk, +1 212 859 1660)) \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7904pjr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
