\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BOJ looking lonely in stand against yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 1999\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 8 (Reuters) - Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market into accepting a weaker yen for now, but dealers and analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fighting an increasingly lonely battle. \par \par \uc2  "I doubt official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be strong enough to overcome the wave of fund flows now coming in to Japan from overseas," said Noriyoshi Tsunoda, chief dealer at Fuji Bank's International Treasury Division. \par \par \uc2  He added that the threat of BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is basically the sole factor supporting the dollar against the yen now. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been able to keep a toehold above 122 yen due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's so-far strong resolve to prevent a rise in the yen from choking off an economic recovery before it starts. \par \par \uc2  But with little incentive to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollar/yen trade has got stuck in a rut as investors hold out for a chance to nudge the dollar down without retaliation by the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market five times since June 10, buying dollars and selling yen to the tune of around $30 billion. The series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped boost the dollar from slightly below 118 yen to around 122.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only have a limited effect in turning sentiment around. They are standing alone, fighting against the majority." said Akio Makabe, senior analyst at DKB Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  One major factor blocking any dollar rise is consistent dollar-selling demand from Japanese exporters, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese exporters have been actively selling dollars in the futures market, taking advantage of its relatively high level after the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With the painful experience of the yen's sudden appreciation last October still fresh in their memories, most Japanese exporters - believing the dollar will resume its fall before long - have fulfilled their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  demands for the next three months, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But exporters, believed to have set their long-term average exchange rate at around 120 yen, are still actively looking for chances to sell to cover their needs until year-end. \par \par \uc2  A finance manager at one Japanese major exporter said: "We expect the dollar to fall in the near future on a likely economic recovery (in Japan), so we have completed most of our required dollar-selling until September." \par \par \uc2  Exporters will be able to convert their dollars back into yen above 120 yen through long-term forward contracts if they sell them above 122.70, given the current five-month dollar/yen forward rate discount of around 2.70 yen including commissions. \par \par \uc2  "Around 123 yen is a satisfying level for us and we are taking action in tandem with the BOJ," the manager said. \par \par \uc2  Continuing strong capital inflows on the back of growing optimism that Japan's economy is on the mend are also working against the dollar, especially after Japan reported an eye-popping first-quarter growth rate of 1.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  Net purchases of Japan stocks by foreigners have been seen each month since last October, according to Finance Ministry data, hitting a peak of 1.6 trillion yen ($13 billion) in March. \par \par \uc2  "Hedge funds are keeping a low-profile and Japanese institutional investors, once aggressive foreign securities buyers, are nervous to take risk. With such limited fund outflows from Japan, the yen is obviously going to get stronger," DKB's Makabe said. \par \par \uc2  ($=122 yen) \par \par \uc2  ((Mariko Hayashibara, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv78050l2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
