\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dollar gains sap European  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 1,082 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 1999\par 17:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 8 (Reuters) - The dollar gave European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a further pounding on Thursday, rising to all-time highs against the euro and multi-year peaks against sterling and the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said there were few signs of inflationary pressures in the United States to weigh on the dollar, and the market's fascination with the euro falling to $1.00 was preventing the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from benefiting from modest signs of growth in the 11-nation euro zone. \par \par \uc2  "The gravity of parity is having a psychological influence," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "That, plus the fact everyone is very shy of buying the euro after the big moves we have seen, means there is no recovery in the euro at a time when growth prospects are getting better." \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to a life low of $1.0149. It was at $1.0173/77 by 1448 GMT, compared with $1.0218/25 in late European trade on Wedenesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell to $1.5536, its lowest since September 1996, and was last at $1.5564/74 versus $1.5595/05 late in Europe on Wednesday. The dollar rose as high as 1.5789 Swiss francs, its highest since July 1991. It is currently at 1.5766/76 from 1.5685/90 in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Our targets are still below parity on euro/dollar," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "We are probably a few weeks away from getting there and I don't believe the ECB will come in before hand. But when you have Eichel saying it is helping Euroland growth it is a red rag to a bear as far as the euro is concerned." \par \par \uc2  The euro hit its life low against the dollar after German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said there was no problem with a weaker euro and it was good for German exports. \par \par \uc2  Wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had helped prop up the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  But many analysts dismissed the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aciton on behalf of the euro. \par \par \uc2  "Whenever we go down below $1.02 we get rumours of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying, but I have no particular feeling that it is there," said Graham Cocks, manager of corporate treasury at Mellon in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, analysts said sterling was continuing to suffer from the prospect that British interest rates could fall below U.S. interest rates later this year for the first time in nearly 15 years. The Bank of England on Thursday kept British rates unchanged at 5.0 percent, level with those of the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "If the Fed raises in August you have got higher short rates in the U.S. than in the UK," said HSBC's Bloom. \par \par \uc2  "Given the history of the rates of return in the two countries, money will probably flow to the U.S. rather than the UK and that is having a heavy influence." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also slightly firmer against the yen, bolstered by fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was at 122.61/64 yen from 122.15/25 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan has been pretty effective and will continue to be," said Barrow at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  "If growth expectations in Japan don't live up to the market's expectations that will give the Bank of Japan a helping hand in weakening the yen." \par \par \uc2  "That, plus the fact everyone is very shy of buying the euro after the big moves we have seen, means there is no recovery in the euro at a time when growth prospects are getting better." \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to a life low of $1.0149. It was at $1.0173/77 by 1448 GMT, compared with $1.0218/25 in late European trade on Wedenesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell to $1.5536, its lowest since September 1996, and was last at $1.5564/74 versus $1.5595/05 late in Europe on Wednesday. The dollar rose as high as 1.5789 Swiss francs, its highest since July 1991. It is currently at 1.5766/76 from 1.5685/90 in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Our targets are still below parity on euro/dollar," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "We are probably a few weeks away from getting there and I don't believe the ECB will come in before hand. But when you have Eichel saying it is helping Euroland growth it is a red rag to a bear as far as the euro is concerned." \par \par \uc2  The euro hit its life low against the dollar after German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said there was no problem with a weaker euro and it was good for German exports. \par \par \uc2  Wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had helped prop up the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  But many analysts dismissed the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aciton on behalf of the euro. \par \par \uc2  "Whenever we go down below $1.02 we get rumours of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying, but I have no particular feeling that it is there," said Graham Cocks, manager of corporate treasury at Mellon in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, analysts said sterling was continuing to suffer from the prospect that British interest rates