\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Dollar/yen vols extend bounce from lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 1999\par 00:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, July 8 (Reuters) - Dollar/yen implied volatilities continued to rebound Thursday, led by rumoured buying interest in yen puts at the front end, after vols earlier this week sank to their lowest levels in two years, U.S. traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said rumours that a bank was buying as much as $1 billion in high-strike yen puts in options dated under a month buoyed implied volatilities at the front end of the curve. \par \par \uc2  "That pushed things up a little bit despite the fact that spot didn't move much today," an interbank broker said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended the day at 122.50 yen after opening near 122.25 yen and rising as high as 122.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen volatility climbed to 9.8/10.0 percent, up from the previous day's 9.2/9.5 percent. On Tuesday, it dropped into the single digits for the first time since early 1997. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen volatilities have been deflating since early June, when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling yen to curtail its strength. \par \par \uc2  One-year dollar/yen volatility was 11.6/11.8 percent, up from 11.4/11.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals continued to see their yen call bias erode in one-month 25-delta options Thursday as the market's appetite for yen puts increased. The risk reversal preference for calls narrowed to a relatively slight 0.2/0.4, in from 0.45/0.65 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar options volatility firmed slightly, to 9.35/9.55 percent in the one-month from 9.2/9.35 percent Wednesday, after the spot euro dropped to a new lifetime low of $1.0149 and then reversed course to finish the day on its highs near $1.0220. \par \par \uc2  The euro came under renewed pressure early when Germany's Finance Minister Hans Eichel said he saw no problem with the euro's low level. European Commission president Romano Prodi later said he was not disturbed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's low level either. \par \par \uc2  But rumours that the Bundesbank had asked German commercial banks to be on alert for possible action on the euro helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover at midday. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bank AG and Commerzbank AG, two of Germany's largest banks, said they were unaware of such a request, and the Bundesbank and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals favoured euro puts by 0.1/0.3 in one-month options and euro calls in the three-month through one-year dates. \par \par \uc2  ((Susan Kelly, Chicago Derivatives Desk 312-408-8750 chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7804th9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
