\par\par \b \uc2  Summers May Use G-7's Balkans Talks To Press Europe, Japan On Growth \b0\par\par\uc2 994 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 1999\par 11:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRUSSELS -(Dow Jones)- A meeting Tuesday on coordinating aid for the Balkans will give newly minted U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers a platform to press European and Japanese finance officials about the need for far-reaching structural reforms in their countries. \par \par \uc2  Finance ministers and senior officials from the Group of Seven countries - the U.S., Japan, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, and the United Kingdom - will attend the Brussels gathering, along with officials from the European Commission, the World Bank, the International Monetary Fund, and other multilateral lenders. The commission, which is the 15-nation European Union's executive body, and the World Bank are co-hosts. \par \par \uc2  This first session of the so-called High-Level Steering Group on Balkan economic reconstruction will lay ground rules for international efforts to get the region back on its feet following the Kosovo conflict. Actual aid pledges will be made later. \par \par \uc2  It will also be Summers's G-7 debut in his new capacity as Treasury chief, although he is no stranger to global policymaking. Previously, as deputy Treasury secretary, Summers was the point man on international issues for former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin. Rubin's G-7 swan song was in Cologne last month. \par \par \uc2  Summers, only a few days into his new job, already has come out slugging against what he perceives as misguided economic policies in Japan and Europe that are pushing the dollar ever higher against the euro and the yen. This causes political problems at home for Summers and the rest of the U.S. administration because it feeds the trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with CNBC Wednesday, the Treasury chief told \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to knock off yen-weakening open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and shift the focus to demand stimulation and deregulation in the corporate sector. \par \par \uc2  He had similar advice for Europe. The focus there "has to be on developing an appropriate domestic growth strategy," Summers argued. \par \par \uc2  Summers said Tuesday he wants to address these issues face-to-face with G-7 partners in Brussels. \par \par \uc2  The Brussels engagement comes at a delicate time for the G-7. The U.S. economy continues to grow, while investor enthusiasm for U.S. assets remains strong despite a quarter-point interest rate hike by the Federal Reserve last week. Whether the euphoria can endure another Fed tightening is the key issue for investors at the moment. But most economists see the U.S. economy holding strong at least through the end of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, activity in the 11-country euro zone and the rest of Europe is patchy. A second-half rebound, predicted by politicians and some economists, still seems more a hope than a certainty. \par \par \uc2  Germany, which accounts for about a third of euro-zone economic activity, is the principle focus of concern. When German June jobless figures were released Tuesday, government officials admitted that the economy still lacks spark, and the jobless rate won't be falling much this year. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, meanwhile, bullish first-quarter growth data fanned hopes that the country may be emerging from a years-long period of economic stagnation. But officials there admit that it is too early to say if the country has turned the corner, while observers argue that lots of structural reform - particularly in the financial sector - is needed to create a sustainable base for activity. Japan's finance minister, Kiichi Miyazawa, isn't expected to be at the Brussels meeting, and the country will be represented by a senior official. \par \par \uc2  Thus, if the U.S. economy starts to slow next year, it isn't certain whether other regions will be in a position to grab the growth baton. The U.S. also is weary of a large trade deficit that reflects the inability of Asia and Europe to absorb a lot of U.S. goods, while the U.S. draws in relatively cheaper imports. \par \par \uc2  If Summers chooses to discuss monetary issues in Brussels - it's not clear yet how much time will be devoted to the matter - the euro is likely to feature. It hit another all-time low Thursday at $1.0168 or 13% below its Jan. 1 launch level of $1.16675. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said they don't plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro, but they also assert that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plenty of room for appreciation. \par \par \uc2  From the U.S. perspective, this monetary mess derives from fundamental factors. Stuart Eizenstat, nominated as the next deputy secretary of the Treasury, said Wednesday the euro needs to be supported by a "more dynamic economy across Europe" to be successful in the long term. \par \par \uc2  As a prelude to the Balkan meeting, euro-zone finance ministers will gather Monday in Brussels for a routine monthly discussion that is expected to focus on budget and taxation issues. They will join up later Monday with counterparts from the U.K., Denmark, Sweden, and Greece for a full meeting of EU finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  Coincidentally, the euro-zone ministers are likely to talk about an agreement reached at a G-7 meeting in Cologne last month that sets out euro-zone representation in the global policy forum. \par \par \uc2  The euro-zone ministers will hear complaints from acting EU Economics Commissioner Yves-Thibault de Silguy and acting Commission President Jacques Santer about the commission's effective exclusion from the euro zone's G-7 lineup. \par \par \uc2  The commission was the fall guy in a concession to the U.S., which has complained about having too many Europeans at the G-7 table following the creation of the euro. \par \par \uc2  While the commissioners may dissent, their scope for disrupting the G-7 agreement is limited because they are leaving office in September. A new commission team led by Italy's Romano Prodi will succeed them. \par \par \uc2  -By Peter Goldstein; 322-285-0130; peter.goldstein@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1111 4212 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv78031vs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
