\par\par \b \uc2  G7 Balkans Mtg Tues Is Summers' Get-To-Know-You Session \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Goldstein \par \uc2 1,093 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 1999\par 11:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRUSSELS (Dow Jones)--A meeting on Balkan aid coordination Tuesday will give newly minted U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers a platform to press European and Japanese finance officials about the need for far-reaching structural reforms in their countries. \par \par \uc2  Finance ministers and senior officials from the Group of Seven countries - the U.S., Japan, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, and the U.K. - will attend the Brussels gathering, along with officials from the European Union Commission, the World Bank, the International Monetary Fund, and other multilateral lenders. The Commission and the World Bank are co-hosts. \par \par \uc2  This first session of the so-called High-Level Steering Group on Balkan economic reconstruction will lay ground rules for international efforts to get the Balkan region back on its feet following the Kosovo conflict. Actual aid pledges will be made at a later date. \par \par \uc2  It will also be Summers' G7 debut in his new capacity as Treasury chief, although he is no stranger to global policymaking. Previously, as deputy Treasury secretary, Summers was the point man on international issues for former Treasury secretary Robert Rubin. Rubin's G7 swan song was in Cologne last month, when the finance ministers met there June 12. \par \par \uc2  Summers, only a few days into his new job, already has come out slugging against what he perceives as misguided economic policies in Japan and Europe that are pushing the dollar ever higher against the euro and the yen. This causes political problems at home for Summers and the rest of the U.S. administration because it feeds the trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with CNBC television Wednesday, the Treasury chief told \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to knock off yen-weakening open market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and shift the focus to demand stimulation and deregulation in the corporate sector. \par \par \uc2  He had similar advice for Europe. The focus there "has to be on developing an appropriate domestic growth strategy," Summers argued. \par \par \uc2  Summers told journalists Tuesday he wants to address these issues face-to-face with G7 partners in Brussels. \par \par \uc2  Doubts Over Economy, Uncertainty On Rates \par \par \uc2  The Brussels engagement comes at a delicate time for the G7. The U.S. economy continues to plough along, while investor enthusiasm for U.S. assets remains strong despite a quarter-point interest rate hike by the U.S. Federal Reserve last week. Whether the euphoria lasts another Fed move - possibly as early as next month - is the crystal ball gazers' question of the moment. But most economists see the U.S. economy holding strong at least through the end of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, activity in the 11-country euro zone and the rest of Europe is decidedly patchy. A second-half rebound, predicted by politicians and some economists, still seems more a hope than a certainty. \par \par \uc2  Germany, which accounts for roughly one third of euro zone economic activity, is the principle focus of concern. When German June jobless figures were released Tuesday, government officials admitted that the economy still lacks spark, and the jobless rate won't be falling much this year. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, meanwhile, bullish first quarter data fanned hopes that the country may be emerging from a years-long period of economic stagnation. But officials there admit that it's still too early to say whether the country has turned the corner, while observers argue that lots of structural reform - particularly in the financial sector - is needed to create a sustainable base for activity. Japan's finance minister isn't expected to be at the Brussels meeting, and the country will be represented by a senior official. \par \par \uc2  Thus, if the U.S. economy finally starts to slow down next year, it isn't certain whether other regions will be in a position to grab the growth baton. The U.S. also is weary of a large trade deficit that reflects the inability of Asia and Europe to absorb a lot of U.S. goods, while the U.S. sucks up relatively cheaper imports. \par \par \uc2  If Summers and company choose to discuss monetary issues in Brussels - it's not clear yet how much time they will devote to this - the euro is likely to feature. It hit another all-time low Thursday at $1.0168 or 13% below its Jan. 1 1999 launch level of $1.16675. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said they don't plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro, but they also assert that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plenty of room for appreciation. \par \par \uc2  From the U.S. perspective, this monetary mess derives from fundamental factors. Stuart Eizenstat, nominated as the next deputy secretary of the Treasury, said Wednesday the euro needs to be supported by a "more dynamic economy across Europe" to be successful in the long term. \par \par \uc2  EU Finance Ministers Meet Monday \par \par \uc2  As a prelude to the Balkan meeting, euro zone finance ministers gather Monday in Brussels for a routine monthly discussion that is expected to focus on budget and taxation issues. They will join up later Monday with counterparts from the U.K., Denmark, Sweden, and Greece for a full E.U. finance ministers' powwow. \par \par \uc2  Coincidentally, the euro-zone ministers are likely to talk about an agreement reached at a G7 meeting in Cologne last month that sets out euro-zone representation in the global policy forum. \par \par \uc2  The euro-zone ministers will hear complaints from acting E.U. Economics Commissioner Yves-Thibault de Silguy and acting Commission President Jacques Santer about the Commission's effective exclusion from the euro zone's G7 lineup. \par \par \uc2  The Commission was the fall guy in a concession to the U.S., which has complained about having too many Europeans at the G7 table following the creation of the euro. \par \par \uc2  While the Commissioners may rant and rave, their scope for disrupting the G7 agreement is limited because they are leaving office in September. A new Commission team led by Italy's Romano Prodi will succeed them. \par \par \uc2  The E.U. finance ministers also are expected to get a preview of the U.K.'s plan to shield the $3 trillion Eurobond industry from the impact of a proposed E.U.-wide withholding tax. \par \par \uc2  U.K. sources told Dow Jones Newswires that the proposal will call for international bonds held in a clearing system to be exempt from the new law. It will also recommend that a further threshhold be established to exempt large denominations of Eurobonds held outside a clearing system. \par \par \uc2  -By Peter Goldstein; 322-285-0130; peter.goldstein@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 08-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0918GMT \par \par \uc2  1111 4212 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7805402\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
