\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Hobbled euro/dollar struggles to rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,082 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1999\par 13:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 7 (Reuters) - The euro was modestly firmer against the dollar by midsession in Europe on Wednesday but seemed incapable of moving too far away from the life lows below $1.02 set a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Better-than-expected German manufacturing orders were unable to offer the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  enough impetus to break higher as continued concerns about European public finances and the lure of parity against the dollar kept pressure on the downside. \par \par \uc2  "We have had some stabilisation and yet the euro still can't make any upside progress," said Rob Hayward, economic adviser at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  "The euro continues to be disappointing in the way it trades." \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0229/33 by 1024 GMT, compared with the life low of $1.0180 set on Tuesday and $1.0221/23 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts did not rule out a slight bounce higher in the short term, before the euro turns lower and heads for $1.0. \par \par \uc2  "What you really need in the short term to sustain a push higher in the euro is a sell off in the Dow. But that has looked pretty impressive since the Fed hike last week," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  Hayward at Bank of America said U.S. asset markets would provide the main focus for the dollar over the next few days. \par \par \uc2  "You probably have to see (U.S.) stocks and bonds moving in one direction in a significant fashion before we can see the dollar pull strongly in either direction," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar remained generally fairly well supported against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with most recent dollar strength seen against sterling. \par \par \uc2  The pound was last at $1.5586/96 compared with $1.5745/55 late in Europea on Tuesday. Sterling fell to $1.5585, its lowest since September 1996 in U.S. trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee has begun its two-day meeting on interest rates, but most analysts were not expecting a change in the 5.0 percent base rate. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile euro/sterling was struggling to push higher having earlier reached 65.73 pence, its highest level in nearly a week. It was last at 65.58/60, compared with 64.93/95 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc, which had been the first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suffer in the most recent bout of dollar strength, was on a more stable footing. It was at 1.5677/82 francs, compared with 1.5695/03 in Tuesday's late European trade. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen continued to trade in a narrow range between 121.80 and 122.20. The dollar was last at 122.10/15 yen versus 122.33/38 in late European trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was at 124.83/87 compared with 125.05/15 in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "People are still concerned about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the possibility of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on their behalf," said Bank of America's Hayward. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Haurhiko Kuroda said overnight Japan was watching dollar/yen and euro/yen rates more closely than in the past. \par \par \uc2  Hayward said there had been some demand for yen from overseas investors and because of Japanese repatriation in recent weeks, but this trend was now beginning to wane. \par \par \uc2  "We are probably building up for another run at the upside," he said. \par \par \uc2 