\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Most Asian markets down, but Hong Kong up. \b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1999\par 07:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 7 (Reuters) - Asian markets were glum on Tuesday, with Tokyo shares off 0.25 percent and Singapore stocks losing two percent as Australia, spooked by a falling gold price, dropped half a percentage point. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong, Korea, Malaysia and Taiwan bucked the trend. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei 225 average had dipped 0.28 percent to 18,000.19 by 0502 GMT, tugged between investors buying stocks to increase weightings in Japan and those taking profits after a recent rally. \par \par \uc2  "The tankan (survey) was a big event and pushed the Nikkei over 18,000. Now we're trying to find a solid footing at this level," said Dhia Bitar, senior institutional sales trader at Nomura Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has been hampered by profit-taking since closing above 18,000 on Monday after the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey showed improved business confidence, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The climb above 18,000 for the first time since September 1997 is an important level of recovery for the index, and a pause at this level is natural, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 122.08/14 yen at 0317 GMT, as lingering wariness over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept sellers at bay, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "As I always say, we are carefully watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. We are carefully watching both yen/dollar and yen/euro movements," said senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng index was up 0.16 percent at 14,394.90 after earlier being driven down by two big share placements and Wall Street's edge away from record territory overnight after a long holiday weekend. \par \par \uc2  "People are starting to think about the possibility of more placements of this size and that the market might not be able to sustain them," said Percy Au-Young, sales director at DBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  Australia's All Ordinaries index fell 0.46 percent to 3,067.6 points as stocks declined across the board and the gold sub-index tumbled about 6.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Gold is just so bad today it has rubbed off onto other sectors," said CIBC Eyres Reed dealer David Massey. \par \par \uc2  New York gold ended sharply lower on Tuesday following the Bank of England's auction of 25 tonnes of the metal. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's Straits Times Index (STI) was down 2.16 percent at 2,128.48 by 0459 GMT, with more pressure expected as optimism about the market rally was waning. \par \par \uc2  "Punters have stopped buying. There are a lot of sellers in the market," one local broker said. "This is hitting market flows. But anyway, the market deserves some cool-down." \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's TAIEX stood up 0.18 percent at 8470.07 points, as investors began to digest the state's market-cooling moves and technologies gave up profits. \par \par \uc2  Heavy institutional buying drove South Korea's Composite Stock Price Index up 1.24 percent at 1001.72 by 0459 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "Investor sentiment had been buoyed by ample liquidity and reports the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had raised its outlook for South Korean economic growth," said analyst Yoon Sam-wi at LG Securities Co. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta's composite index was down 0.68 percent at 676.971 by 0500 GMT, after concerns about politics choked off an early rise that took it to 690.27. \par \par \uc2  Philippine shares were down 0.18 percent at 2605.59, with Malaysian shares up 0.39 percent at 849.56. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7704xeq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
