\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar/Yen's Inability To Rally Seen Holding Tsys Back \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephanie Hoo \par \uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1999\par 20:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar's perpetual inability to rally against the yen may be curbing Japanese investors' appetites for U.S. Treasurys, at a time when bonds are generally pressured by improving global growth. \par \par \uc2  When overseas investors put money in dollar-based assets, they do so in the hopes the dollar will strengthen -- or at least remain stable -- against their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the dollar has shown a tendency to weaken against the yen this year and especially this past month, as fund managers plow money into rallying Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  For Japanese investors putting money in Treasurys, "the risk is, the value of the foreign asset is going to fall," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist at NatWest Global Financial Markets in New York. "It doesn't take much of an exchange rate move" to wipe out returns, he added. \par \par \uc2  For example, the 10-year Japanese government bond is yielding 1.65% Wednesday afternoon, and the 10-year U.S. Treasury yields 5.90%. \par \par \uc2  That means the dollar would only need to fall 4.25% against the yen -- from the current Y122.20 to Y117.00 -- to wipe out Japanese investors' Treasury gains. \par \par \uc2  If it weren't for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against yen strength, the dollar could easily fall as low as Y110.00, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Treasurys have been edging lower on supply concerns and the ongoing strength of demand-led U.S. growth. \par \par \uc2  "Buyers are hesitating until the smoke clears," said Sung Won Sohn, chief economist at Wells Fargo in Minneapolis, Minn., adding that he expects the 30-year Treasury yield to head to 6.25% by September from the 6.07% Wednesday afternoon, as the selling continues. \par \par \uc2  "If it weren't for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar would be a lot weaker. Investors look at all these things," Sohn said. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line is, many Japanese seem to think the U.S. stock market has peaked," he added. And if U.S. interest rates are headed up and Japanese stocks are climbing to 20-month highs, "Why go overseas when you can stay home?" Sohn said. \par \par \uc2  Preliminary data show Japanese investors bought a net Y732 billion in overseas bonds in May, down from Y2.777 trillion in April, though the difference may be exaggerated by seasonal factors such as the start of Japan's fiscal year on April 1. \par \par \uc2  The exchange rate's importance to investment flows has prompted speculation that the BOJ's yen-weakening may be specifically engineered to help Japan's big investors get out of overseas investments before they lose value in yen terms. \par \par \uc2  "Domestic investors have suffered serious mishaps since 1995," Bhagavatula said, citing years of declines in Japanese stocks and real-estate prices. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be a tacit way on the part of the BOJ to tell investors, 'Don't double up on your losses'." \par \par \uc2  James McCormick, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan in New York, agrees the BOJ action may be part of a "greater purpose" of not only supporting Japan's export-led growth but also helping its financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  But McCormick adds that even though institutions are cashing out of old positions, the BOJ's support of dollar/yen actually makes dollar-based assets more attractive -- not less attractive -- for Japanese investors to put on new positions. \par \par \uc2  Plus, "we think the Japanese economy will weaken in the next three quarters, including the quarter that just passed. If the data shows this, the BOJ doesn't need to do much to keep dollar/yen from falling," McCormick said. Even so, improving global growth might make it difficult for the dollar to rally much higher than Y130.00 this year, he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's inability to climb confounds U.S. bulls who've been saying all year that strong U.S. economic fundamentals should pull the dollar up. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, officials in Japan have steadfastly said a weaker yen helps the nation's nascent recovery. The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  five times in the past month for upwards of $20 billion to prop the dollar and euro up against the resurgent yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday afternoon in New York, the dollar was worth Y122.22, just\par 3.9% higher than the Y117.60 traded when the BOJ began \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  June\par 10 and still down sharply from Y138.65 a year ago.\par    -By Stephanie Hoo; 201-938-4384; stephanie.hoo@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1829GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4215 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv77052j1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
