\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro/dollar off life lows, still soft. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 1999\par 13:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 6 (Reuters) - The euro gingerly clawed back above $1.02 after slipping to a new lifetime low in European trading on Tuesday, taking another step towards the $1.00 level. \par \par \uc2  Confidence in the beleaguered single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was further undermined by talk in Asia overnight that the euro zone's budget deficit limit could be relaxed, while prospects for continued U.S. economic growth set the market's sight on parity. \par \par \uc2  "I think the (euro) bears are closing in very fast now on a test of parity," said David Brown, chief economist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0212/17 at 1035 GMT after slipping to $1.0186 earlier - its lowest since its launch at the start of the year - against $1.0221/23 late on Monday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Brown said the sell-off might have been a little overdone, given it was based on an "impossible" story about undoing the European economic and monetary union stability pact. \par \par \uc2  "It's very easy to jump on the bear bandwagon but...I think the market is overreacting," he said, referring to a newspaper report that the French Planning Commission suggested the pact should be loosened. \par \par \uc2  "That is not going to happen," Brown said. \par \par \uc2  The head of the Commission, Jean-Michel Charpin, later told Reuters that the report did not challenge the EMU stability pact or the need for strict budgetary discipline. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro appeared to be on track to test the $1.00 level, although expectations were growing that European officials would step up their verbal support of the euro, and perhaps step into the market eventually. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer said at a symposium in Tokyo that he was "absolutely confident" the euro would be a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that he would be concerned if euro/dollar moved beyond fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "We're approaching levels where it might make sense for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Minoru Ozawa, head of trading at Sakura Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Had they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at higher levels, it probably would not have stopped the trend - if it did, it would not have helped Europe's economic growth," he said. "At these levels or slightly below parity, I don't think they would mind a little bounce in the euro." \par \par \uc2  In the near term, possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/yen was a more real threat, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen stood at 124.67/71 versus 125.05/15 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Fear of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the dollar pinned near the 122 yen level, with the market's memory of Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying still fresh. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 122.15/20 yen, down from 122.33/38 late on Monday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported heavy selling interest from Japanese exporters and hedge funds above the mid-122s, which was capping the dollar's advance. \par \par \uc2  "When there's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a lot of day traders are happy to go along with the flow. But that's only short-term," Ozawa said. "Supply/demand is in favour of the yen at higher levels." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was broadly stronger against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rallying to a new eight-year high against the Swiss franc and a 23-month high against the pound. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/Swiss was at 1.5709/14 after rising to a high of 1.5741 earlier from 1.5695/03 late in Monday's European trading. \par \par \uc2  Sterling sagged to a low of $1.5675 before recovering marginally to around $1.5682/87 against $1.5745/55 late on Monday. Euro/sterling stood at 65.08/10 pence against 64.93/95. \par \par \uc2  UK May industrial production was slightly better than forecast, up 0.1 percent from April and down 0.4 percent from a year earlier, but the numbers did little to shore up the pound. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0212/17 at 1035 GMT after slipping to $1.0186 earlier - its lowest since its launch at the start of the year - against $1.0221/23 late on Monday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Brown said the sell-off might have been a little overdone, given it was based on an "impossible" story about undoing the European economic and monetary union stability pact. \par \par \uc2  "It's very easy to jump on the bear bandwagon but...I think the market is overreacting," he said, referring to a newspaper report that the French Planning Commission suggested the pact should be loosened. \par \par \uc2  "That is not going to happen," Brown said. \par \par \uc2  The head of the Commission, Jean-Michel Charpin, later told Reuters that the report did not challenge the EMU stability pact or the need for strict budgetary discipline. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro appeared to be on track to test the $1.00 level, although expectations were growing that European officials would step up their verbal support of the euro, and perhaps step into the market eventually. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer said at a symposium in Tokyo that he was "absolutely confident" the euro would be a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that he would be concerned if euro/dollar moved beyond fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "We're approaching levels where it might make sense for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Minoru Ozawa, head of trading at Sakura Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Had they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at higher levels, it probably would not have stopped the trend - if it did, it would not have helped Europe's economic growth," he said. "At these levels or slightly below parity, I don't think they would mind a little bounce in the euro." \par \par \uc2  In the near term, possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/yen was a more real threat, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen stood at 124.67/71 versus 125.05/15 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Fear of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the dollar pinned near the 122 yen level, with the market's memory of Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying still fresh. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 122.15/20 yen, down from 122.33/38 late on Monday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported heavy selling interest from Japanese exporters and hedge funds above the mid-122s, which was capping the dollar's advance. \par \par \uc2  "When there's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a lot of day traders are happy to go along with the flow. But that's only short-term," Ozawa said. "Supply/demand is in favour of the yen at higher levels." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was broadly stronger against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rallying to a new eight-year high against the Swiss franc and a 23-month high against the pound. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/Swiss was at 1.5709/14 after rising to a high of 1.5741 earlier from 1.5695/03 late in Monday's European trading. \par \par \uc2  Ste