\par\par \b \uc2  OUTLOOK-Reuters Asia Business at 0745 GMT. \b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 1999\par 09:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Following are some of the main business news stories (all times GMT) \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  FINANCIAL MARKETS \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Asian markets modestly lower or flat as investors use a Wall Street holiday to cash in on recent gains with Tokyo stocks down 0.6 percent after the previous day's 21-month high. \par \par \uc2  ** Update after main Asian markets close \par \par \uc2  LONDON - Euro hits a record low of $1.0191 and European stock markets seen dipping at the opening after Monday's merger-fuelled rally; dollar holds around 122 yen supported by the threat of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but traders say fact this has not happened yet contributed to euro's weakness. \par \par \uc2  ** Spot, merit updates \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BRITISH GOLD AUCTION \par \par \uc2  LONDON - Markets await Bank of England's first auction of gold in a series of planned gold sales that will reduce its gold reserves by more than half; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  receives bids (until 1030) for sale of 25 tonnes from existing reserves of 715 tonnes. \par \par \uc2  ** Spot coverage and leads \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  ECB'S NOYER CONFIDENT ON EURO \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer tells a Tokyo symposium on euro he is confident it will be a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , repeating the ECB is not interested in a weak euro. At the same time, the euro plumbs new life lows against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  ** Focus on Noyer's and other speakers' comments \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  ENERGY-PETRONAS \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR - Malaysia's national oil firm Petronas reports a 32 5 percent drop in net profit on the back of depressed oil prices but expects a stronger performance in the current year. \par \par \uc2  ** Story on Petronas's results and prospects \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  ECONOMY-SINGAPORE \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Singapore Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong says government will revise its growth forecast in August since economy recovering faster than anticipated. \par \par \uc2  ** Government comments on growth and analysts' expectations for revision in forecast \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  PAPUA/CHINA-TAIWAN \par \par \uc2  BEIJING - China denounces Papua New Guinea's diplomatic recognition of Taiwan, demanding the South Pacific nation "correct its erroneous decision". \par \par \uc2  ** Updates on Chinese anger \par \par \uc2  ** Updates from TAIPEI \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  THAILAND POLITICS \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK - Thailand, whose battered economy is on the mend, plans talks next month with the International Monetary Fund to determine the agency's future role in the country. \par \par \uc2  ** Interview with premier Chuan Leekpai. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KOREA-BEER \par \par \uc2  SEOUL - Coors Brewing Co of United States wants South Korea's Oriental Brewery Co disqualified from auction to take over Jinro Coors for breaking rules and says it may pull out of South Korea if it loses bid. \par \par \uc2  ** Story on Coors effort to acquire former joint venture partner in Korea's highly competitive beer market \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WORLD ECONOMY \par \par \uc2  SAN SEBASTIAN - The world economy should grow at 2.5 percent or more this year as crisis-hit Latin America and Asia are recovering faster than expected, the head of the International Monetary Fund says. \par \par \uc2  ** Merit updates \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  If you have any queries, please call: \par \par \uc2