\par\par \b \uc2  Euro drops to new low of $1.0191 in Tokyo-bankers. \b0\par\par\uc2 223 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 1999\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 6 (Reuters) - The euro dropped to a lifetime low of $1.0191 in late afternoon trade on Tuesday in Tokyo after initial weakness failed to trigger any official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Selling was led by overseas fund operators aiming to trigger stop-loss sell orders around $1.0200," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro briefly dropped as low as $1.0184 and 124.25 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Confidence in the euro was undermined as there has been no indication from European officials, including those who are in Tokyo now, that they are seriously concerned about the euro's weakness," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Christian Noyer, vice president of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), told a Tokyo symposium on the euro on Tuesday that despite fluctuations since its launch in January, the euro's recent levels are broadly consistent with those of the euro's constituent \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the summer of 1997. \par \par \uc2  But the possibility of euro-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  or by the ECB on behalf of the BOJ was making operators cautious about selling the euro too aggressively, dealers said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv76053hs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
