\par\par \b \uc2  Outgoing'Mr Yen'to markets - Mum's the word. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 1999\par 22:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 6 (Reuters) - Japan's "Mr Yen" has a new message for financial markets: "Don't ask me." \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara won't formally hand over his post as the nation's top financial diplomat to his soft-spoken successor Haruhiko Kuroda until Thursday, but he's already been rehearsing for a new role out of the daily limelight. \par \par \uc2  Asked on Tuesday to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Sakakibara told reporters: "I will not make any more comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Please ask Kuroda." \par \par \uc2  It was the second day running that Sakakibara, long known as "Mr Yen" for his influence in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, had deferred to his junior colleague. \par \par \uc2  On Monday he left it to Kuroda, the Finance Ministry's International Bureau chief, to confirm that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars and to reinforce Tokyo's message it does not want to see the yen rise too far, too fast. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the uncharacteristic shyness was intended to tell markets that they should now look to Kuroda for clues to Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and assure them that the policy would not change just because Sakakibara leaves. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line is that there will be no change in policy," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Sakakibara was not the one setting policy, he was part of the equation in the ministry. That's the message they are trying to give in this changeover week, that policy won't change." \par \par \uc2  Japan has been waging a lonely battle to prevent yen strength from crippling its economic recovery before it really begins, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  five times in less than a month. Tokyo is keen to keep hopes that the nation has finally turned the corner in its long economic nightmare from boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too far. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara was never the sole architect of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, but he has certainly been its best-known articulator since rising to the post now held by Kuroda four years ago. \par \par \uc2  That Japan's best-known bureaucrat is unlikely to fade from the scene was clear later in the day when he delivered his views on the future of Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a symposium. \par \par \uc2  Markets, however, were unimpressed by his prediction of long-term strength for the embattled euro, which sank to a lifetime low against the dollar shortly after Sakakibara predicted its medium-to long-term strength. \par \par \uc2  Many expect Sakakibara to keep a low profile while the scholarly Kuroda, respected by those who know him but a lesser-known quantity for market punters, establishes his own credentials. \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda is market savvy but he's an unknown quantity to people in the markets," said one financial diplomat. \par \par \uc2  "He's not unknown to those who deal with the ministry on a regular basis, but at this point he doesn't carry the same weight with the markets. He has to establish his bona fides." \par \par \uc2  While the personnel change spells mostly a difference in style rather than content, some experts say markets could be calmer once Sakakibara bows out. \par \par \uc2  "Sometimes he shot from the hip and either went too far or what he said was interpreted as going beyond what he meant and there was an uncontrolled reaction," the financial diplomat said. \par \par \uc2  "His departure may not be bad for those who want to take a bit of the volatility out of the U.S.-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  That said, few expect Sakakibara, who will follow his predecessors' example by becoming an adviser to the ministry, to remain out of the fray forever. \par \par \uc2  "I'd be surprised if he stays quiet," Landon said. "He'll be useful to put out a message and sometimes it's ex-MOF (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) officials who are able to move the market." \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv760517u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
