\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BOJ wins yen battle, but war not over. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 1999\par 10:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 5 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan (BOJ) again showed its determination to keep the yen on a tight leash on Monday, buying the dollar at ever higher levels to send the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply lower. \par \par \uc2  But while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's show of strength made the market cautious about buying dollars, Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said it had done little to change the yen's basic bullish mood amid growing signs of a domestic economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has given a clearer message after today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now the market feels that the bank will not allow the dollar to fall below 120 yen," said Takashi Toyahara, deputy manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  "Also, the BOJ may have wanted to give the impression that it doesn't mind the dollar strengthening from current levels," Toyahara said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar would have risen anyway following Monday's somewhat weaker-than-expected results of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "tankan" corporate survey, but its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at hammering home the message that it does not want a strong yen above 120 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market had detected the BOJ stepping in to sell yen shortly after the release of the tankan data in early trade. \par \par \uc2  The tankan diffusion index for big manufacturers improved to minus 37, compared with minus 47 in the previous survey. That was slightly lower than a Reuters consensus forecast of minus 36. \par \par \uc2  The index is the percentage of firms reporting favourable business conditions minus those reporting unfavourable conditions. A negative reading means pessimists outnumber optimists. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was seen buying dollars around 122 yen in Tokyo afternoon trade, bankers said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also rumoured buying above 122.50 yen in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Senior Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda confirmed during the morning that Japan had entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "A premature strengthening of the yen's exchange rate is not appropriate. Today we took action in the market," he said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was first spotted buying dollars at between 121.50 yen and about 121.80 yen in the morning. It was later detected buying dollars above 122 yen, and as high as about 122.30/40 yen. \par \par \uc2  At 0645 GMT, the dollar stood at 122.41/50 yen after climbing to a Tokyo session high of 122.69 yen on Monday. It was at 120.92/02 yen in late New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was well-supported by late afternoon, but its rise was being checked by heavy sales from Japanese exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will limit aggressive speculative sales, but the market will not bid dollars on rallies. The BOJ will continue to be the key buyer, but without bids from other players its gains will be limited," a senior European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "There are still no clear fresh buying factors (for the dollar), despite seeing recoveries in U.S. asset markets. It seems people are still more interested in the uptrend in the Nikkei and signs of improvement in the Japanese economy," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ set a single-month record volume in dollar-and euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry announced that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external reserves ballooned by a record $22.718 billion to $246.337 billion in June from May after a series of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is still interested in yen-buying factors. As long as this mood persists, the BOJ could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily again in July just like we've seen in the last month," the dealer said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv75051y4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
