\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: No End In Sight To Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 820 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 1999\par 08:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--According to Julian Robertson, one of the most famed hedge fund managers in the business, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market forever. \par \par \uc2  But following the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest action on Monday morning, it certainly looks as if the authorities in Tokyo are prepared to continue buying dollars indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  Dissection of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's likely motives in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen reveals few reasons why it should stop, and several compelling arguments why it should continue. \par \par \uc2  If the objective behind the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were really to cement an emerging economic recovery by easing exchange-rate pressures on Japanese exporters, then Tokyo's central bankers would long ago have patted themselves on the back for finishing the job. \par \par \uc2  According to Monday's Tankan report, Japan's large manufacturers are budgeting for an average exchange rate for the current fiscal year of Y116.78 per dollar. So far the yen has barely approached that level since the fiscal year began in April. Today, following three weeks of repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth over $20 billion, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is around 4% weaker than the predicted average for the whole year. \par \par \uc2  But the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still buying dollars. And by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the yen is already looking fairly weak, as it did Monday morning, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is plainly indicating that it wants to see a softer, rather than a more stable, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Preemptive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Paves Way For Fiscal Expansion\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Taichi Sakaiya, the head of Japan's Economic Planning Agency, let slip\par part of the explanation on Sunday when he told a Japanese television\par program that the government will have to implement an additional\par supplementary budget this fiscal year, on top of the Y520 billion extra\par job creation budget already on the table.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Targeted at topping up dwindling government spending programs in the\par last months of the fiscal year, any additional supplementary spending\par package is likely to be significantly larger than the job creation\par mini-budget proposed currently.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts predict that a second supplementary budget will add up to\par trillions, rather than billions, of yen, and fiscal expansion of that\par order of magnitude will only strengthen the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the Bank of Japan having effectively ruled out quantitative\par monetary easing to offset fiscal expansion, the rise in long-term\par interest rates caused by the higher level of bond issuance needed to\par fund fiscal expansion would be enough by itself to boost the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par So by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen preemptively, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par creating a comfort zone. The closer toward Y130 it can push the dollar,\par the better Japanese exporters will be able to cope with a 10% rally in\par the yen triggered by a fresh round of fiscal expansion later in the\par year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Lessons of 1994 Point Toward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Now\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rest of the explanation behind \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lies in the\par U.S., an economy Japanese officials believe to be severely overheated.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Skeptical about the new paradigm and nervous that the U.S. stock\par market is overdue for a deep and lasting correction, Japanese\par policy-makers are keen to avoid repeating the events of 1994.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Then, a rise in U.S. interest rates triggered an abrupt sell-off in\par the U.S. bond market, which in turn initiated a sell-off in the dollar,\par propelling the yen all the way to Y80 to the U.S. dollar a year later.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Keen to discourage a repeat of the yen's rally and its disastrous\par consequences for corporate Japan should a sell-off in U.S. equities\par follow last week's Fed rate hike, Japanese policy-makers regard\par preemptive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen as a doubly attractive\par strategy in current market circumstances.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Given the added benefit that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen allows\par Japanese institutional investors to cut, or at least hedge, loss-making\par positions in euro-denominated assets, the Bank of Japan is unlikely to\par be overly troubled that its unilateral action has failed to attract the\par support of the U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Both the Fed and the ECB have shown themselves to be deeply reluctant\par to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets except when they perceive exchange\par rates are getting dangerously out of line with economic fundamentals.\par The Bank of Japan, however, has always been readier to act in support of\par domestic policy imperatives.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And bearing in mind that the current round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does\par appear to be driven by the demands of domestic policy-makers, rather\par than the need to correct any temporary aberration in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets, there is little reason to expect it to stop any time soon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 05-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0621GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7504zge\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
