\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Shaky euro eyes life lows vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1999\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 2 (Reuters) - The euro remained vulnerable near its recent lifetime lows against a broadly firm dollar on Friday ahead of key U.S. jobs data due for release later on the day. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Thursday's fall to a new life low of $1.0201 underscored the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's failure to win the markets' trust or to reverse the downward trend seen since its launch. \par \par \uc2  "There is a good chance we will retest the lows ahead of U.S. payrolls today," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at ABN AMRO in London. "When people saw how little support the euro had when it was heading to $1.03, that was a sign that the market had no confidence and it just accelerated the fall." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro had little ammunition to counteract the exuberance of the dollar. The dollar set a 23-month peak of $1.5699 against the pound on Friday and climbed to an eight-year high of 1.5720 against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts said the continuation of the dollar's upward trend was helped by the thinness of the market ahead of the release of U.S. data and before a U.S. holiday on Monday. They added the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was helped by strong U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "Nothing is going to stop euro/dollar falling unless the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on euro/yen," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  By 0800 GMT, euro/dollar was quoted at $1.0215/20 against $1.0237/42 late in Europe on Thursday. Euro/yen had fallen to 123.61/65 from 123.72/77 late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders the markets remained nervous after Thursday's rumours that central banks were selling yen for euros, with some dealers saying the Bank of Japan was checking rates in euro/yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara declined on Friday to comment on the rumours. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the BOJ was interested in protecting the June life lows in euro/yen of 122.53, but would be reluctant to act against the market trend. \par \par \uc2  "They do not like to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the market and if euro/dollar is in freefall there is very little they can do about it," said Gunner. \par \par \uc2  He added that the BOJ would probably keep off the market until the release of the quarterly Tankan survey on July 5. \par \par \uc2  The majority of economists interviewed in a Reuters poll said they expected the Tankan report to show an improvement in business confidence, but they said they thought the Japanese economy was not out of the woods yet. \par \par \uc2  Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya also said on Friday that Japan's economy was not yet on a recovery path. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 121.07/12 yen, little changed from 121.09/12 yen late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar was squeezed in very tight ranges, supported by wariness of the BOJ, but capped by offers above 121 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they were keeping their eyes fixed on the U.S. payrolls data for indications on whether the current dollar's strength was going to continue. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters said they expected the U.S. non-farm payrolls to rise 220,000 in June, after an 11,000 increase in May. \par \par \uc2  "If we see a very strong number, then we would get a sell-off in U.S. bonds and stocks, and that would potentially be negative for the dollar," said Ravi Bulchandani, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merril Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  "If the data come out at consensus or below, that would just vindicate the Fed's policy stance and take stocks and the dollar up," he added \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the weakness of the euro combined with a surge in oil prices had spilt into the Norwegian crown, dragging the single European unit to a new lifetime low of 8.0600 crowns. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7205776\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
