\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dlr rises with little help from Fed. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 1999\par 15:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 1 (Reuters) - The U.S. Federal Reserve's decision on Wednesday to raise interest rates by quarter of a percentage point will have little long-term impact on the dollar, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to eight-year highs against the Swiss franc and reached record highs against the euro in European trade on Thursday, but analysts said the hike to 5.0 percent in the federal funds rate target was not contributing much to the dollar's rise. \par \par \uc2  "The decision to hike rates was expected and had no impact whatsoever, while the Fed's move to a neutral bias was, if anything, negative for the dollar," said Brian Kiely, treasury analyst at Royal Bank of Scotland. "But the dollar is pushing ahead on its own steam as U.S. fundamentals are looking good." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the fact the Federal Reserve had removed its mention of a tightening bias in the statement accompanying its verdict on rates had diminished, but not erased, expectations for further U.S. rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  "There's been no shift of sentiment in the dollar," said Andrew Snowball, senior economist at Julius Bear Investments in London. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted that in the past the Fed had always acted gradually on rates and preferred to shift to a more neutral stance on monetary policy before a tightening, leaving scope for a further hike maybe already in August. \par \par \uc2  "In the statement there's nothing which prevents the Fed from raising rates again, especially if Friday's payrolls data are strong," said Marco Pianelli, senior strategist at Nomura. \par \par \uc2  The consensus forecast is for June non-farm payrolls to rise by 220,000, from a previous gain of 11,000. \par \par \uc2  But even if there is another rate hike analysts said further decisive dollar rallies were unjustified in the long-term, in view of a gradual improvement of the economic conditions in Europe. He cited a recent batch of benign euro zone data, as shown by Italy's stronger-than-expected Q1 GDP and an improvement in business sentiment in June. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters euro zone Purchasing Managers' Index, released on Thursday, grew in June at the fastest rate since September, rising to 51.5 on an adjusted basis from 50.5 in May. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, economists said that the growth rate seen in U.S. GDP in the first two quarters of 1999 were unsustainable and were predicting a less torrid economic growth in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "Earlier in the year the big surprise was the strength of the U.S. economy. Now things could go the other way around," said Klaus Kusber, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "The narrowing of the T-note/Bund spread reflects the thinking that the Fed will bring down the growth rate in the U.S. over the next couple of months and economists are getting more optimistic about Europe," Kusber said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was likely to remain stable above the 120 yen threshold amid fears that the Bank of Japan could once again stick its fingers in the market to curb any excessive yen strength, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 120.93/03 yen at 1222 GMT, little changed from 120.95/05 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar rose to record highs around $1.0246 from $1.0316/23 late on Wednesday, after German Economics Minister Werner Mueller said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was right to protect price stability, but must also support the economy. The ECB left rates unchanged on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also gained against the Swiss franc, to 1.5620/30 from 1.5524/34 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reutes.com)). \par \par \uc2  "The shift to neutral bias signals that the Fed isn't half as concerned as the market had perceived about the threat of inflation coming through in the second half of the year." \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost ground against all European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but its losses against the yen were limited by persistent fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There is still underlying wariness that the BOJ might step in," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. "But the BOJ will be looking at the mood of the market rather than at a specific level, and at the moment the market is calm." \par \par \uc2  By 0800 GMT the dollar had eased to 120.71/81 yen from 120.95/05 in late European trading on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials Eisuke Sakakibara and Haruhiko Kuroda reiterated on Thursday that there was no change in the the MOF policy and that the ministry would continue to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments closely. \par \par \uc2  BOJ policy board memeber Kazuo Ueda also said that worries over mid-to-long term outlook for the Japanese economy remained and that a further yen fall would relieve Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  He added it was not time for the BOJ to consider ending its zero interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar stood at $1.0351/56 compared with $1.0316/26 late in Europe on Wednesday. The euro had risen as far as $1.0388 in early Europe, its highest in four days. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that the euro had benefited from the decision of the Fed to shift to a neutral bias on monetary policy because it was now anticipating a slowdown in U.S. growth after the strong performance of the first two quarters of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Now that the FOMC meeting was out of the way, analysts were taking into account recent positive European economic data such as Italy's stronger than expected first quarter GDP growth and an improvement in French business confidence. \par \par \uc2  "The market is more optimistic on Europe and not so bullish on the U.S. economy and this could lead to a recovery of the euro and a little bit of downside correction of the dollar," said Klaus Kusber, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index grew in June at the fastest rate since September, rising to 51.5 on an adjusted basis from 50.5 in May. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell from overnight peaks set against both sterling and the yen. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen stood at 125.24/37 against 124.78/89 late in Europe on Thursday but below its three-day top of 125.97 set overnight. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling quoted 65.53/59 pence, slightly up from 65.47/49 late on Thursday and off a four-day peak of 65.75 set earlier in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  The market was awaiting the result of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board meeting, which is expected to leave rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/Swiss had eased from Wednesday's eight-year peak of 1.5565 francs but was recovering some of the ground lost overnight, while sterling was in the meantime clinging to its gains and testing a resistance level at $1.58. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7105e11\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
