\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar underwhelmed by Fed's mild mannered hike. \b0\par\par\uc2 772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 1999\par 06:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 1 (Reuters) - The dollar held its ground against the yen after a thin Thursday morning session, but only thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears and it was down on most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted the dollar had failed to attract new buying despite the relief rally in U.S. asset markets in the wake of the Federal Reserve's mild mannered rate tightening. In contrast, foreign funds continued to pour into Japanese stocks, resulting in a natural upward bias for the yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also beneficiaries of the Fed's restraint, courtesy of narrowing yield differentials and an improved outlook for global growth. \par \par \uc2  By 0345 GMT the dollar was idling at 120.85/91 having dipped from New York's late 121.17/22 in one early lunge following the Fed's move to raise key rates to 5.0 percent from 4.75 percent. Analysts noted much had been made of the Fed's shift to a neutral policy stance, whereas history suggested keeping a tightening bias would have been the real surprise. \par \par \uc2  In 1994 when the Fed was in the process of hiking rates from three percent to six percent it adopted a neutral bias after every single increase. The market's relief was rather a reflection of how desperately bearish it had become on the policy outlook, believing two or more hikes were a foregone conclusion. \par \par \uc2  The money market is still pricing in another 25 basis point tightening, but analysts are more confident now that anything more will require the fear of inflation to be confirmed by economic data. \par \par \uc2  That change of view saw U.S. yields decline sharply, while Japanese and euro-area yields are on a rising trend, due in part to expectations of a pick-up in growth in these long moribund economies. \par \par \uc2  Signs that the Japanese economy has at least bottomed have attracted offshore funds into equities, where there is also the hope of higher earnings as corporate Japan restructures. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei celebrated the start of the new quarter with a rally of more than two percent, reaching a new 21-month high of 17,972 and bringing its gains so far this year to almost 30 percent. This rise has done much to improve bank's balance sheets and may eventually feed into improved consumer confidence, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  "Much could still go wrong, and probably will, but all the market's doing is unwinding some of the ultra-bearish positions taken on Japan in recent years," said one dealer at a European bank. "That trend favours the yen and is far more fundamental than the MOF (Finance Ministry) would like to admit." \par \par \uc2  Which was why he expected the yen to push higher next week, particularly if Monday's tankan survey of Japanese business sentiment was as upbeat as some expected. \par \par \uc2  But he added that the market would be wary of taking the dollar under 120.00 yen because of the risk of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The latest reserve data suggests the BOJ spent more than $25 billion restraining the yen last month, so there was no doubting its resolve, he said. \par \par \uc2  There was no such danger on the euro which crept up to $1.0386/91 by the midsession from $1.0330/37 here on Wednesday, breaking resistance at 1.0370 in the process. \par \par \uc2  Commodities and commodity-related \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also gained as the Fed's modest move seemed to pose no threat to the U.S. economy or to on-going recovery in world growth. \par \par \uc2  Copper put in a stellar performance as three month futures jumped over seven percent to a nine month peak, while oil made a 19-month high. \par \par \uc2  All this helped the Australian dollar break through tough resistance around 66.50/60 U.S. cents to stand at 66.91/96 from 65.90/95 on Wednesday, and bullish technicals pointed to a test of the year high around 67.45 soon. \par \par \uc2  One \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  heading the other way on the dollar was the Swiss franc. It has been in a long decline as the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has grappled with a weak economy by slashing rates and encouraging a depreciation to make exports more competitive. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the Swiss franc sank to its lowest level since August 1991 at 1.5566 per dollar and although it had bounced back a little to 1.5435 by the midsession, dealers suspected a test of the 1991 trough at 1.5937 was inevitable. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7105chw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
