\par\par \b \uc2  Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Reserves Show 'Mr. Yen' Leaves With A Bang \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Fowler, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 1999\par 15:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- A day after formally announcing the retirement of its top field marshal for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  released data showing the old soldier went out with a bang. \par \par \uc2  The ministry said Japan's reserves of convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights increased by $22.72 billion in June - a record monthly rise - to $246.38 billion. \par \par \uc2  Since reserves tend to increase by around $2.0 billion each month due to interest payments, the figures indicated that Japan purchased about $20 billion during the month to keep the dollar above Y120.00 and support the euro. \par \par \uc2  It was a fitting finish to the public career of Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara, who will step down from office in early July. In the four years since Sakakibara rose to the forefront of Japan's international policy team - earning the nickname "Mr. Yen" from traders - Japan has repeatedly bought and sold the yen to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  Most of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has involved selling yen for dollars. Exactly four years ago, Japan's reserves totaled $157.03 billion. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's data also reflected the recent shift in capital flows that has accompanied growing hopes among investors that Japan's economy may have hit bottom. \par \par \uc2  "The large size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  really underlines the point that there were large inflows of capital into Japan last month. It took $20 billion just to offset those inflows," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  went against the fundamentals. Without it, the dollar would probably be at Y115 right now," Landon added. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the yen has picked up in recent weeks after the government released data showing the economy grew at a surprisingly strong 7.9% annualized pace in the January-March quarter. Rising interest rates amid notions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might abandon it's ultra-easy monetary policy also have bolstered the yen. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami helped fuel such speculation Wednesday, when he said in parliament that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't be able to continue its current zero-rate policy forever. He also said he'd consider "the next step" after thorough study of the economic situation. The bond market took Hayami's remarks as a sign that the BOJ might raise interest rates in the near future. \par \par \uc2  Thursday, a member of the BOJ's policy board sought to repair the damage. At a press conference, BOJ policy board member Kazuo Ueda said the bond market misunderstood Hayami's remarks. \par \par \uc2  "The governor was talking about what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy stance would be at a time of normal economic growth. But the governor also said the current economic situation isn't in a normal state. What he meant to say, I think, was that (the BOJ) will keep the current monetary policy." Ueda said, \par \par \uc2  Still, many analysts remain bullish about both Japan's economy and the yen, suggesting Sakakibara's battle to keep the yen weak may ultimately end in defeat. The dollar is currently quoted at Y120.82. In attempting to keep the dollar above the Y120 level, Japanese financial authorities purchased the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at levels as high as Y122.20-30 last week. \par \par \uc2  Hiroshi Saito, general manager of the international treasury at Mitsubishi Trust & Banking Corp., said he expects the dollar to meet resistance on any rises. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar goes up (against the yen), Japanese investors will be sellers," Saito said. \par \par \uc2  Landon expects the dollar to fall to Y115 by the end of July - "barring any more massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  No one doubts \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ministry officials have repeatedly warned of their readiness to take action if the yen rises before the economy is strong enough to withstand the move. \par \par \uc2  In that sense, Thursday's data also underscored the escalating efforts by countries to build up war chests to fend off attacks on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or strengthen their influence on international markets. \par \par \uc2  A European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said at a conference in the U.S. last weekend that the ECB is seeking to stock its own \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  arsenal. The bank is considering a proposal to double its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $100 billion, the ECB official said. Analysts in the U.S. said the move could be an attempt to emulate Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market influence. The ECB's main pool of reserves, which currently total some $50 billion, are for the purpose of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2