\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen bulls frolic ahead of FOMC. \b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 1999\par 17:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 29 (Reuters) - The euro fell to its lowest level against the yen since the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros for yen on behalf of the Bank of Japan on June 18, putting the market on guard for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar also reached its lowest level against the yen since the most recent round of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 21, as dealers dared to push the market down ahead of the two-day U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting which begins on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "There are worldwide capital inflows into Japan," said a trader from a German bank in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "The Nikkei is rising, the Japanese property market is stabilising, but the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is there," he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro dropped through key supports at 125.20/30 and 125 to trade at 124.83/96 yen at 1440 GMT, nearly one yen below levels of 125.74/80 reached in late Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen fell below 121 to 120.79/84, from 121.15/19 yen late on Monday, but kept above strong support at the June 21 low of 120.55. \par \par \uc2  The yen looked attractive after the Nikkei hit its highest 1999 close on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The FOMC meeting also provided a window for traders to drive the yen higher, as they said Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not anticipated ahead of the FOMC's decision, expected at 1815 GMT on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar and U.S. assets were unlikely to react if the FOMC raised U.S. interest rates as expected, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed is going to raise by 25 basis points, and retain a bias to tighten," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read. "But I am not sure how much of a followthrough there is going to be - the fixed income and equity markets are pricing in a 50 basis point rise by the end of the year." \par \par \uc2  U.S. assets and therefore the dollar might benefit from an immediate 50 basis point rise in the 4.75 percent federal funds rate, as a large, swift move would remove further rate rise expectations from the market, Crise added. \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, was poised to strengthen against the dollar if it could battle against strong technical resistance, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  A downward-sloping trendline drawn from the euro's launch-day peak of $1.1906 provided resistance at $1.0420 on Tuesday.The euro was trading at $1.0325/28 at 1501 GMT, from $1.0384/86 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Klaus Kusber, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt, said a recent tightening in the 10-year U.S./German yield spread was a positive sign for the euro. \par \par \uc2  The spread narrowed from a 10-year peak around 180 basis points last week to 150 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is pricing in the idea that the growth differential between the U.S. and Europe will narrow," said Kusber. \par \par \uc2  The euro could rise to strong resistance at $1.0550, which has capped its gains in the last month, Kusber said. \par \par \uc2  The euro gained against the Swiss franc on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  It reached one-month highs at 1.6027 before subsiding to 1.6008/13 francs at 1510 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also rose against the Swiss franc, stopping short of 1.55 francs. It reached a near-eight-year high of 1.5545 last week. \par \par \uc2  Swiss National Bank officials have repeatedly said they want to see the Swiss franc stable against the euro, but SNB vice chairman Jean-Pierre Roth recently said the dollar's rise against the franc was no cause for concern." \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6t05hki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
