\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar buoyant as FOMC draws nearer. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,082 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1999\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 28 (Reuters) - The dollar was firm against the euro by mid-session in Europe on Monday, erasing much of the weakness seen at the end of last week. \par \par \uc2  Speculation grew last week that the Federal Open Market Committee might raise U.S. interest rates by as much as 50 basis points at this week's meeting. But analysts said a smaller quarter point hike was now more likely and expectations were that a smaller hike would lift U.S. assets and help the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Today's moves (in the dollar) are partly compensation for some of the moves seen at the back end of last week," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "People are taking a step back and thinking the selling last week was overdone." \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was at $1.0394/99 by 1108 GMT, compared with Friday's peak of $1.0480 and $1.0457/61 late in European trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had pulled back from one-week lows against the yen and was at 121.24/29 versus a low of 121.05 and compared with 121.45/50 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Front month S&P futures were up 2.5 points on the day, suggesting the Dow Jones Industrial's Index would open steady to higher and this was underpinning the dollar, analysts said. On Friday the Dow closed up slightly on the day at 10,552.56. \par \par \uc2  David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber in London, said it was still unclear whether any rate hike this week would be followed up with more interest rate increases in the months ahead. He added that this would hold the key to the dollar's outlook. \par \par \uc2  "The next FOMC meeting is in two months time and if the data in that two months show the U.S. economy is slowing it will be slightly negative news for the dollar because it makes it less likely interest rates will have to rise further," he said. \par \par \uc2  Near term, analysts said, the dollar was supported against the yen amid wariness that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry officials Eisuke Sakakibara and Haruhiko Kuroda warned overnight that Japan would take decisive action on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They also said that the moves in dollar/yen at towards the end of last week had been too rapid. \par \par \uc2  "People are not going to tolerate too much dollar/yen weakness given the threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  A devlauation of the Columbian peso on Monday was also used as fodder for FOMC speculation and cited as a reason for the improved performance of the dollar in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  modified its exchange rate regime, opening the way to a maximum de facto peso devaluation of 10.85 percent. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the devaluation had spurred speculation that the FOMC was more likely to raise rates by a quarter-percentage point rather than the half-point rise traders had begun to talk about last week sparking a rise in the dollar on the back of anticipation U.S. assets would be bolstered but a smaller cut. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed against sterling and the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  The pound was last at $1.5821/31, compared with a high of $1.5871 and $1.5897/02 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/Swiss was at 1.5395/05, up almost one percent from Friday's near two week lows. \par \par \uc2  "People are taking a step back and thinking the selling last week was overdone." \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was at $1.0394/99 by 1108 GMT, compared with Friday's peak of $1.0480 and $1.0457/61 late in European trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had pulled back from one-week lows against the yen and was at 121.24/29 versus a low of 121.05 and compared with 121.45/50 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Front month S&P futures were up 2.5 points on the day, suggesting the Dow Jones Industrial's Index would open steady to higher and this was underpinning the dollar, analysts said. On Friday the Dow closed up slightly on the day at 10,552.56. \par \par \uc2  David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber in London, said it was still unclear whether any rate hike this week would be followed up with more interest rate increases in the months ahead. He added that this would hold the key to the dollar's outlook. \par \par \uc2  "The next FOMC meeting is in two months time and if the data in that two months show the U.S. economy is slowing it will be slightly negative news for the dollar because it makes it less likely interest rates will have to rise further," he said. \par \par \uc2  Near term, analysts said, the dollar was supported against the yen amid wariness that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry officials Eisuke Sakakibara and Haruhiko Kuroda warned overnight that Japan would take decisive action on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They also said that the moves in dollar/yen at towards the end of last week had been too rapid. \par \par \uc2  "People are not going to tolerate too much dollar/yen weakness given the threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  A devlauation of the Columbian peso on Monday was also used as fodder for FOMC speculation and cited as a reason for the improved performance of the dollar in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  modified its exchange rate regime, opening the way to a maximum de facto peso devaluation of 10.85 percent. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the devaluation had spurred speculation that the FOMC was more likely to raise rates by a quarter-percentage point rather than the half-point rise traders had begun to talk about last week sparking a rise in the dollar on the back of anticipation U.S. assets would be bolstered but a smaller cut. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed against sterling and the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  The pound was last at $1.5821/31, compared with a high of $1.5871 and $1.5897/02 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2      Dollar/Swiss was at 1.5395/05, up almost one percent from \par \par \uc2   Friday's near two week lows. \par \par \uc2     ((Deepthi Wickremasinghe, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 \par \par \uc2  4040, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6s05mr2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
