\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar underpinned before FOMC. \b0\par\par\uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1999\par 17:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 28 (Reuters) - The dollar was firm against the euro at the end of a quiet European session on Monday, and was kept above 121 yen by fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar remained trapped in narrow ranges throughout the day as dealers were reluctant to put on new trades before the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting on Tuesday and Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Speculation grew last week that the FOMC might raise U.S. interest rates by as much as 50 basis points this week. But analysts said a smaller quarter point hike was now more likely. \par \par \uc2  "A 25 basis point rise would be a relief for the asset market and in turn positive for the dollar," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  "But anything above 25 would hamper the dollar," he added, saying that a bigger rate hike would hurt the U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  By 1440 GMT euro/dollar was at $1.0384/86, compared with Friday's peak of $1.0480 and $1.0457/61 late in European trade. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial's Average was up more than one percent at 10,667.18, suggesting the market was now less inclined to believe the Fed would increase rates by 50 basis points. \par \par \uc2  The euro had attempted an advance against the dollar earlier in the session but failed to sustain gains above $1.0430. \par \par \uc2  "People are still scared of being long on the euro," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. "Unless the euro moves decisively above $1.0550, the market will not gain confidence." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the dollar had recovered from one-week lows versus the yen hit in overnight Asian trading, helped by wariness that the Bank of Japan might step in again to curb the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  By 1445 GMT dollar/yen was at 121.15/19 against a day's low of 121.05 yen and 121.45/50 yen late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry officials Eisuke Sakakibara and Haruhiko Kuroda warned overnight that Japan would take decisive action on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said that verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials, now seen almost on a daily basis, was becoming less and less effective. \par \par \uc2  They added that the market might take advantage of thinning trading volume before the FOMC meeting to push the yen higher. \par \par \uc2  "Between now and the FOMC we'll see the yen creeping towards 120 yen," said Andrew Milligan, director of economic research at Morley Fund Management in London. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities might step in, but it is probably sensible for them to wait and see what the Fed does, which will give them a clearer picture on how much action they actually need to take. \par \par \uc2  A devaluation of the Columbian peso on Monday was also used as fodder for FOMC speculation and cited as a reason for the improved performance of the dollar in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  modified its exchange rate regime, opening the way to a maximum de facto peso devaluation of 10.85 percent. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the devaluation had initially spurred market talks that the Federal Reserve would raise rates by a quarter percentage point rather than half a point. They added that the impact of the news was nevertheless short-lived. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rallied against the Swiss franc, pushing above the 1.54 barrier and gaining around one percent from Friday's near-two-week lows. \par \par \uc2  The broad-based firmness of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the pound as well. The British unit was dragged down towards a major support at $1.58. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was last at $1.5818/28 against a session peak of $1.5871 and compared with $1.5897/02 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6s05ihd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
