\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar near lows, but supported by wariness of BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1999\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 28 (Reuters) - The dollar was crawling near the day's lows near 121 yen in late Tokyo trade on Monday, but persistent wariness of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented players from selling actively, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Interbank operators unloaded dollars late in the session after they were caught long early, when the dollar edged up on yen selling against the euro linked to purchases of shares in a Deutsche Telekom AG offering, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dipped as low as 121.05 yen, but operators were reluctant to chase it down further. At 0634 GMT, it stood at 121.13/16 yen compared with 121.43/53 in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  At 0647 GMT, the euro stood at $1.0423/25 compared with $1.0422/30 in late New York trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Some interbank operators who went long the dollar because they thought its early gains toward 122 yen were on bids from the BOJ were busy unwinding those positions by the afternoon," a city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Early rounds of euro buying against the yen indirectly pushed the greenback as high as 121.95 yen, but the uptrend waned quickly after the market determined the gains were linked to Japanese investor buying of Deutsche Telekom shares. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Telekom on Sunday set a price of 39.50 euros a share for 285.9 million shares sold in a secondary offering. \par \par \uc2  The issue met with strong demand from private and institutional investors worldwide, with 60 million shares ordered in the United States and Japan alone, Telekom Chief Executive Ron Sommer told a news conference in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the payments for the share offering to occur at the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  They added that the dollar was underpinned against the yen by comments from Japanese monetary officials. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said on Monday that the ministry is always ready to take decisive action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, in a statement given to reporters at the Finance Ministry, said: "Market moves toward the end of last week were very rapid. Our determination to avoid premature yen strength is firm." \par \par \uc2  There was scant reaction to confirmation that Sakakibara was to be replaced by senior MOF official Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  "Sakakibara's departure will have limited impact in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the near term. I don't expect dollar/yen to be shaken by this," a senior city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But we want to see how the market reacts to Kuroda's comments in the future," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Sakakibara is very unique and sometimes his comments really shocked the market, but people still listened to him. I don't think Kuroda is the type who would surpise or shock the market. Kuroda seems more quiet," the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  modified its crawling peg exchange rate regime on Sunday, opening the way to a maximum de facto devaluation in the embattled peso of 10.85 percent, under standard international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  calculations. \par \par \uc2  But the reaction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in Tokyo was limited, with many dealers focusing closely on how overseas markets absorb the news. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered the central parity of the peso's trading band by nine percent against the dollar. It also widened the upper and lower limits of the band to allow a maximum daily fluctuation of 20 percent, compared with 14 percent at present. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at the scale of the economy, I don't think a Colombian devaluation would shake up the global financial markets. The initial reaction was pretty much limited in Tokyo," said a dealer at a European bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But we have to see how the New York market absorbs this news. Dealers are sensitive, particularly as the market waits for the results of the FOMC meeting this week," the dealer said. ((Chikafumi Hodo, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6s05h2e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
