\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-Japanese Finance Ministry Official Denies BOJ Has Yen Target Zone \b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1999\par 04:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- A former top Japanese Finance Ministry official said Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dogged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar against yen wasn't aimed at keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a specific trading range or "zone." \par \par \uc2  Japan has come under increasing international pressure to revive its sluggish economy, mired in its deepest recession since the end of World War II. Japan's economy is still struggling to shake off the effects of a plunge in land prices in the early 1990s, which buried the country's banks under a mountain of failed or risky loans. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen hurts Japanese exporters -- the main engine of the economy -- by making their products less competitive. That's why the Bank of Japan sold yen on June 10, when it reached a seven-week high near 117.50 to the dollar on a report showing Japan's unexpected economic growth in the January-March period. \par \par \uc2  Japan has bought $15 to $20 billion in the dollar-yen market alone this month, selling its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 10, 14 and 21. In addition, the Bank of Japan had the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase euros on its behalf June 18 and bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own last Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar doesn't have the momentum to keep rising because Japanese exporters are eager to sell dollars for yen to bring profits home, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "(Monetary officials) don't have particular levels, such as 120 yen, or whatever in mind," Kosuke Nakahira, a former vice finance minister for international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  "So far as the market moves gradually, they don't care as long as it reflects moves in the real economy," said Nakahira, who is currently vice chairman of the private Institute For International Economic Studies. \par \par \uc2  The repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar was aimed at sending a strong message to the market that Japan doesn't want a premature rise in the yen to short circuit an economic recovery, Nakahira said. "They want to impress their determination that in the early stages of the economic recovery, they don't wish to see rapid moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  Precipitating the yen's surge, he said, was the release in early June of surprisingly strong gross domestic production data showing that the economy expanded at an annual pace of 7.9% in the January-to-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  The market "over-reacted" to the news, pushing the yen up sharply versus the dollar and causing long-term interest rates to rise, he said. "Authorities were very much worried that there were symptoms of over-reaction in the market," Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's determined effort to keep the yen in check may also be an attempt to get the jump on the market before the release of its "Tankan" quarterly survey of business sentiment, he said. The Tankan, set for release July 5, is expected to show that business sentiment is improving. \par \par \uc2  Other reasons for the "pre-emptive" strike included possible yen-bullish market reaction after the heads of state from the Group of Seven declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following their meeting in Cologne. \par \par \uc2  Nakahira noted that markets tend to react to remarks - or the lack thereof - on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the case of the Cologne summit, markets might have tried to push the yen up after realizing that G7 leaders had decided to forgo the opportunity to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  In addition, a quick yen rise could also undermine consumer confidence, as such moves would be reported in the media as hurting exports and thereby the recovery, he said. \par \par \uc2  "That would give consumers more caution. They're wondering if (GDP growth) is going to continue in the second quarter or not," he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A rapid increase in the yen also would hurt exporters and the\par economy, but if moves were to come at a gradual pace, exporters could\par adjust, Nakahira said.\par    -James Simms, 813-5255-2940; jsimmsap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv6s02z7x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
