\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Japan Isn't Expected To Change Low Interest-Rate Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1999\par 04:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Economic data released Friday put a damper on market hopes that Japan might have begun to put deflationary concerns in the past. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the Bank of Japan isn't any closer to abandoning its near-zero interest-rate policy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  While the numbers in the consumer price and wholesale price indexes weren't all bad - some indexes inched slightly higher - what improvements there were came mostly from one-time factors, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "These aren't data that would make the Bank of Japan feel that deflationary pressure is easing," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at Sanwa Securities. \par \par \uc2  The data showed that while consumer prices in Tokyo dropped 0.4% in June on year and fell 0.2% from May, the seasonally adjusted CPI rose 0.1% from May. The Tokyo core CPI, which excludes fresh foods, was flat on month and down 0.1% on year before adjustment. Adjusted, the core CPI for Tokyo rose 0.1% on month and was flat on year. Meanwhile, domestic wholesale prices rose 0.1% in the second ten days of June from the previous ten days but fell 1.6% on year. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the Bank of Japan might abandon its current policy was partly to blame for a sharp rise in short-term interest rates last week. The March 2000 three-month Euroyen futures yield jumped to 0.47% Friday, the highest it's been since the Bank of Japan adopted in February its current policy of driving short-term rates to near zero. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan has repeatedly said it will continue its ultra-easy monetary policy until deflationary worries are gone. Friday's data showed there has been no respite from deflation despite the recent recovery in prices for some commodities. One reason, analysts say, is that the yen is more than 15% stronger than it was last year at this time. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen would tend to put deflationary pressure on the economy because it pushes down import prices. For that reason, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves would force the Bank of Japan into a holding pattern on monetary policy, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  While recent dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , estimated to total at least $20 billion, has weakened the yen somewhat from levels a few weeks ago, the rallying Japanese stock market and growing bullishness about the economy is expected to keep upward pressure on the yen from here. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's closely-watched Tankan survey of business sentiment, due out on July 5, is expected to trigger yen-buying if it shows dramatic improvement in sentiment, analysts said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted the survey just after the government announced surprisingly strong gross domestic product data for the January-to-March period. \par \par \uc2  Restructuring by Japanese companies is also expected to weigh on prices, as corporate lay-offs and salary cuts make consumers less willing to spend. \par \par \uc2  "Since there is pressure on the yen to strengthen, and because companies will continue to cut prices to stay competitive, deflationary pressure is likely to persist," said Toshiyuki Suzuki, chief economist at Sanwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  Japan's debt markets were initially spooked in early June by the strong GDP data, which showed that Japan's economy expanded at an annualized pace of 7.9% in the January-to-March quarter. But analysts said that those figures weren't enough to convince the Bank of Japan that demand is picking back up. They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains concerned about how the huge gap between excessive supply and weak demand will affect prices from here. "The Bank of Japan is even more cautious than commonly perceived," Sanwa Securities' Saito said. While markets are speculating a change in policy, "the Bank of Japan is just sitting back." \par \par \uc2  Naomi Hasegawa, senior economist at Tokyo-Mitsubishi Securities, said, "Making adjustments to the zero interest rate policy is not on the Bank of Japan's agenda right now. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We expect the Bank of Japan to continue to keep its zero interest\par rate policy throughout this year and next year, unless there is a risk\par of inflation," she added.\par -By Kenneth McCallum; 813-5255-2943; kmccallumap.org\par -By Kenji Hall; 813-5255-2939; kmhallap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv6s02z7o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
