\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr capped vs yen at Tokyo midday on euro/yen fall. \b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1999\par 07:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 25 (Reuters) - The yen's rise against the euro kept the dollar under pressure below 122 yen at Tokyo midday on Friday, but caution over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities ensured solid support for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's downside was limited with bids detected at 121.70-80 yen on buybacks by dealers who had sold in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Behind these buybacks was caution over possible dollar buying by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) as verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued on Friday morning and also because the BOJ last stepped into the markets on Monday and boosted the dollar to above 122.50 yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 121.75/80 yen at 0330 GMT, off an early high of 122.10 yen and New York's close of 121.83/93 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was little changed against the dollar. It was at $1.0414/17 at 0335 GMT against New York's $1.0408/11. \par \par \uc2  But the euro fell to around 126.80 yen by midday on selling from Japanese importers and investors after it rose above 127 yen overnight, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The euro/dollar was spotlighted overnight amid lack of activity in dollar/yen recently, and gains in that pair boosted euro/yen to the level that eventually attracted Japanese selling," said a senior trader at a major city bank. \par \par \uc2  "Thus, dollar/yen was under downward pressure this morning," he said. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar was well underpinned above 121.50 yen, the level that the BOJ is possibly aiming at protecting, as several stop-loss orders were rumoured near that level, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Comments by senior MOF officials on Friday morning provided some support to the dollar as they ruled out market talk of a possible collapse in Japan-U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation. \par \par \uc2  "There is caution over BOJ's possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on one hand. On the other hand, there are growing doubts about \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation between Japanese and U.S. authorities and recent selloffs in U.S. asset markets," said a trader at another major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said on Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) was determined to prevent a premature rise in the yen and that cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  between Japan, the United States and Europe was going well. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's views on cooperation with the United States were echoed by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa. Asked for his views on recent reports that U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin appeared not to be supportive of Japan's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, Miyazawa said it had not been confirmed to whom Rubin made such comments or whether they were even made in the first place. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa added: "Our cooperation will not be torn apart." \par \par \uc2  Asked whether he meant that there would be joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Miyazawa said, "I'm not saying that. I'm not thinking about it (such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )." \par \par \uc2  Traders said, however, MOF officials' comments were not strong enough to wipe out persistent speculation that Washington may be opposed to further BOJ dollar-buying as that could heighten trade tensions between the two nations. \par \par \uc2  "(Despite Miyazawa's comments,) the dollar/yen is basically stuck in the current range," said the second dealer. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Australian dollar rose above the $0.66 barrier on Friday as copper prices were lifted by news that Autralia's Broken Hill Pty would cease its U.S. copper \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's cable and today's Aussie are the favourites of some traders who were fed up with the range-bound dollar/yen," said a trader at a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie was at $0.6622/27 at 0445 GMT against around $0.6590 just before the BHP announcement on Friday morning, which indicated the possible alleviation of a forecast oversupply in the copper market. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.5870/80, little changed during Tokyo's morning session from $1.5878/88 at Thursday close in New York. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3 3432 8570 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6p05j6t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
