\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-Aide At Japan's Finance Ministry Sees No Target For Dollar-Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Simms, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1999\par 16:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- A former top official at the Finance Ministry said Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dogged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar against the yen wasn't aimed at keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a specific trading range or "zone." \par \par \uc2  Monetary officials "don't have particular levels, such as 120 yen, or whatever, in mind," Kosuke Nakahira, a former vice finance minister for international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets four times in the last two weeks in an effort to stem - or preempt - an appreciation of the yen against the dollar and the euro. Market participants estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $20 billion in yen over the period. \par \par \uc2  "So far as the market moves gradually, they don't care as long as it reflects moves in the real economy," said Nakahira, who is currently vice chairman of the private Institute For International Economic Studies. \par \par \uc2  The repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar was aimed at sending a strong message to the market that Japan doesn't want a premature rise in the yen to short circuit an economic recovery, Nakahira said, adding, "They want to impress their determination that in the early stages of the economic recovery, they don't wish to see rapid moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  Precipitating the yen's surge, he said, was the release in early June of surprisingly strong gross domestic production data showing that the economy expanded at an annual pace of 7.9% in the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  The market "over-reacted" to the news, pushing the yen up sharply against the dollar and causing long-term interest rates to rise, he said. "Authorities were very much worried that there were symptoms of over-reaction in the market." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determined effort to keep the yen in check may also be an attempt to get the jump on the market before the release of its quarterly survey of business sentiment, known as the "tankan, "he said. The survey, set for release July 5, is expected to show that business sentiment is improving. \par \par \uc2  Other reasons for the "preemptive" strike included possible yen-bullish market reaction after the heads of state from the Group of Seven declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after their meeting in Cologne, Germany, last weekend. \par \par \uc2  Nakahira noted that markets tend to react to remarks - or the lack thereof - on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the case of the Cologne summit, markets might have tried to push the yen up after realizing that G-7 leaders passed on an opportunity to comment on exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  In addition, a quick yen rise could also undermine consumer confidence, as such moves would be reported in the media as hurting exports and thereby the recovery, he said. "That would give consumers more caution. They're wondering if (GDP growth) is going to continue in the second quarter or not," he said. \par \par \uc2  A rapid increase in the yen also would hurt exporters and the economy, but if moves were to come at a gradual pace, exporters could adjust, Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  -James Simms, 813-5255-2940; jsimms@ap.org \par \par 