\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED -  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro firmer against dollar, sterling. \b0\par\par\uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1999\par 11:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In LONDON story headlined \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro firmer against dollar, sterling, please read in first paragraph ....The euro was firmer against the dollar in early European trading on Thursday, helped by its rally to four-week highs against sterling.... instead of ....21-month highs (correcting time period). \par \par \uc2  A corrected story follows \par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 24 (Reuters) - The euro was firmer against the dollar in early European trading on Thursday, helped by its rally to four-week highs against sterling and light short-covering after Wednesday's downside probe failed to reach new lows. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to a high of $1.0370 after drifting just above $1.03 for most of overnight trading. It had dipped below $1.03 on Wednesday to within a quarter cent of its $1.0254 life low. \par \par \uc2  "We tested the lows but it was well supported, so everyone is now just waiting for the FOMC (the June 29-30 Federal Open Market Committee meeting)," a dealer at a Japanese bank in London said. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0355/60 at 0807 GMT against $1.0310/15 in Wednesday's late European session. \par \par \uc2  The uptick in euro/dollar mirrored a rise in euro/sterling to a four-week high of 65.76 pence, compared with 65.32/38 late on Wedensday, and a one-centime pullback in dollar/Swiss franc to 1.5425/30 from Wednesday's 1.5545 eight-year high. \par \par \uc2  The pound was broadly weaker, hitting a 21-month low of $1.5750/60 against the dollar for the second consecutive day after the minutes of the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee's June meeting released on Wednesday fanned speculation of lower UK interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0355/60 at 0807 GMT against $1.0310/15 in Wednesday's late European session. \par \par \uc2  The uptick in euro/dollar mirrored a rise in euro/sterling to a four-week high of 65.76 pence, compared with 65.32/38 late on Wedensday, and a one-centime pullback in dollar/Swiss franc to 1.5425/30 from Wednesday's 1.5545 eight-year high. \par \par \uc2  The pound was broadly weaker, hitting a 21-month low of $1.5750/60 against the dollar for the second consecutive day after the minutes of the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee's June meeting released on Wednesday fanned speculation of lower UK interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's minutes made it clearer that there's a chance of another rate cut in the UK, whereas we look for at least a 25-basis-point increase by the Fed next week," said Michael Rottmann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. "That would translate into a crossing of (U.S./UK) short-term interest rates, and this should give some pressure on cable." \par \par \uc2  Analysts expected euro/dollar to consolidate for the time being, probably testing lows again eventually. \par \par \uc2  "The directional skew for euro/dollar is still toward the downside," said David Brown, chief European economist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "I still think the overriding theme for the market is going to be parity ($1.00 to the euro). This is not going to go away." \par \par \uc2  Over the long term, the interest rate differential between the U.S. and Europe would continue to favour the dollar against the euro, he added. \par \par \uc2  Should the Fed raise rates by 50 basis points instead of 25 as most people appeared to be anticipating, the differential would be larger, while a quarter-point move would likely fuel a recovery rally in U.S. asset markets, Brown observed. \par \par \uc2  "It's kind of a no-lose situation for the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was pegged near 122 yen as lingering fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmly supported the downside, while exporters and investors had hefty selling interest above 122, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 121.87/92 yen at 0905 GMT against 122.02/07 late in Wednesday's European trading. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese bank dealer said end-user sell orders were stacked up from 122.30 yen and above, discouraging speculative buying. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, Japanese Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  guru, Eisuke Sakakibara, repeated his recent remarks that he was carefully watching dollar/yen and euro/yen rates - a thinly veiled warning to the market of the authorities' intention to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a sharp yen rise. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami also repeated his recent line that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would maintain its easy monetary policy as long as there was concern about deflation. \par \par \uc2  ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6o05vht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
