\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Appears To Stand Alone In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Twaronite and Brian Fowler \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1999\par 13:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Washington and Tokyo appear to be reading from different pages when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy these days. \par \par \uc2  In the last two weeks, while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the dollar and euro, the U.S. has been conspicuously removed from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market upheaval. \par \par \uc2  When Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara called reporters together Monday to point out that a tripartite \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  system was in place - implying that coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be in the works - Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin responded later in the day by saying: "I don't know what he means." Rubin pointed out instead that it's important for Japan to pursue structural reforms for its economy. \par \par \uc2  "I think (Japan is) isolated and acting alone" in its recent moves to weaken the yen, said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  It's unclear whether there is actually a rift developing between the world's top financial policymakers on the desirability of pushing down the yen. But some analysts think the tone could harden if the dollar rises much further at a time when the U.S. trade deficit is rising and the Japanese economy remains weak. \par \par \uc2  With Rubin set to step down from office July 4, some analysts believe the markets may already be feeling the influence of incoming U.S Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  "Summers is not a strong dollar policy official to the extent that Rubin is," Landon said."Summers is more amenable to the protectionist point of view. He thinks the deficit is a bad thing, whereas Rubin thought it was a positive aspect of the U.S. economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  The attitude of the U.S. could reflect the belief that weakening the yen isn't the most desirable way to support Japan's economy, some said. A weaker yen without any other domestic demand stimulus would only exacerbate trade imbalances, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly, it would have a major effect (on trade tensions). Maybe not if the yen remains in the 120's, but certainly if there is a continued program which leads to more yen weakness," said James D. Malcolm, economist at J.P. Morgan Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One thing is certain, the world's leading policymakers remain concerned about global imbalances. In their communique last week, G8 heads of state renewed their "commitment to pursue appropriate macroeconomic policies and structural reforms. These will contribute to more balanced growth in the world economy, thereby reducing external imbalances." \par \par \uc2  Japan's economy, the world's second largest, has suffered two straight years of contractions. Largely as a result of that, Japan's trade surplus rose to a record high in the fiscal year ended March 31. The U.S. trade deficit also widened to a record amount last year. \par \par \uc2  Clearly, Japan remains a key source of the global imbalance. \par \par \uc2  European officials have also failed to voice much support for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market activities. Though the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros for yen, that was at the behest of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  And Deutsche Bundesbank President-designate Ernst Welteke has appeared to pour cold water on the BOJ's activities by saying that "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the market is useless." \par \par \uc2  The lack of overt support from counterparts in the U.S. and Europe has forced Japanese financial authorities to step up their rhetoric in recent days. \par \par \uc2  In an interview published in The Nihon Keizai Shimbun's Wednesday morning edition, vice finance minister for international affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said he wants to "get rid of the market perception that the dollar has an upper limit." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's deputy, financial bureau head Haruhiko Kuroda, echoed that statement to reporters as he arrived at work Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  It's possible Japan will be able to go it alone, some said. Since Tokyo has been selling yen, there's no concern that reserves could run short due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's easier to weaken your own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than to strengthen it. While strengthening usually requires coordinated policy, (Japan) doesn't necessarily need the Fed to keep the yen at the current levels," said Brian Rose, senior economist at Warburg Dillon Read. \par \par \uc2  -By Lisa Twaronite (813-5255-2929; ltwaronite@ap.org) \par \par \uc2  -By Brian Fowler (813-5255-2929; bfowler@ap.org) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-06-99 \par \par \uc2  1124GMT \par \par \uc2  1138 4254 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6n04sb2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
