\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Tokyo Volatilities Up On Dollar Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1999\par 04:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Volatilities implied by Japanese yen and euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options are higher in Tokyo Wednesday compared with late Tuesday's New York levels, reflecting a more active spot market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has traded in a relatively wide range against the yen, between a low of Y121.69 and a high of Y122.29. Early in the session, institutional investors speculating that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again bought dollars with the aim of taking profits if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  No \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ensued, but the dollar and euro both rose against the yen after strong words from Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara. He said: "I am closely watching both the dollar/yen and the euro/yen. If we think it is appropriate, we are ready to take decisive measures at any time." \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday in New York, dollar/yen volatilities fell to their lowest level in a year amid practically inert trading. Concern that the BOJ would re-enter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets prevented market participants from taking new positions. Over the past two weeks, the BOJ has spent an estimated $20 billion to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options imply volatility of 11.0% bid, up from 10.8% in New York Tuesday, but below 11.35% in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, they are also higher at 9.3% from 9.2% in both New York and Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Although euro/dollar trading is usually relatively inactive in the Asian session, dealers said volatilities were pushed up slightly on the concern that sharp movements in euro/yen and dollar/yen will bump the euro into a new range against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Other than the cross trading concerns, there are no new factors affecting (euro/dollar spot trading)," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  At 0250 GMT, the dollar was at Y121.99, up from Y121.74 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $1.0345, up from $1.0325 Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One-month risk reversals are biased toward yen calls at 0.5% and euro\par calls at 0.2%. Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either\par put or call options by the premium or discount applied.\par    -By Lisa Twaronite, 813-5255-2929; ltwaronite@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-06-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0257GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6n04q68\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
