\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo stocks limp lower as exporters retreat. \b0\par\par\uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1999\par 09:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 23 (Reuters) - Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei average retreated on Wednesday, as export-oriented companies that have been gaining on the back of the weakening yen suffered profit-taking, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei 225 average fell 190.87 points or 1.07 percent to close at 17,586.75. September Nikkei futures finished 190 points lower at 17,590. \par \par \uc2  "Large-cap stocks that had rallied in the past fews days were hit by profit-taking," said Kokichi Kumagae, senior manager at New Japan Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "But interest in bargain-hunting was still strong and checked the Nikkei from sliding sharply," he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a fall in U.S. stocks overnight, particularly technology issues, helped trigger profit-taking in export-oriented high-tech makers in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the tech-laced Nasdaq composite index slid nearly two percent, ending a one-week surge. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, Fujitsu Ltd  lost 65 yen or 2.72 percent to close at 2,325 and NEC Corp  fell 32 yen or 2.14 percent to 1,462, both in heavy trade. \par \par \uc2  High-flying electronics giant Sony Corp  closed 280 yen or 2.11 percent lower at 13,020. \par \par \uc2  These companies have advanced sharply recently as the decline in the yen has sparked hopes of better earnings. A weaker yen increases the competitiveness of their goods in world markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no obvious sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday morning, but \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara's comment that authorities stood ready to act on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels when appropriate served to keep the yen in check. \par \par \uc2  Traders said investor interest in picking up stocks on dips remained firm due to underlying expectations that Japan's recession-hit economy is improving. \par \par \uc2  "The market should firm up as underlying sentiment is bullish on hopes that the economy is improving," said Masaaki Higashida, general manager of investment information at Nomura Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  But investor outlooks were mixed. Putnam Investment's Tim Ferguson told Reuters Television it plans to reduce the weighting of Japanese stocks in its portfolios as Japan's economy is still flagging despite unexpectedly strong growth figures for the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  "We feel that the first quarter GDP number was probably an aberration," he said. "We expect it (growth) to continue to be negative in the year 2000." \par \par \uc2  A total of 627.85 million shares changed hands on the first section of the Tokyo Stock Exchange, against 612.57 million shares on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Broader indices were mixed. The TOPIX index\tab    of all first-section shares fell 16.46 points or 1.15 percent to 1,409.18. The Nikkei 300\tab    dipped 3.55 points or 1.26 percent to 279.15. But the second section index\tab    gained 19.87 points or 0.96 percent to end at 2,085.92.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Decliners outpaced advancers 894 to 334, with 94 issues unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Elsewhere in the market, some automobile stocks fell, with Honda Motor Co  down 70 yen or 1.29 percent at 5,370 and Mazda Motor Corp  down 36 yen or 4.89 percent at 700.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's "Big Five" automakers saw their exports drop in May from a year earlier on weaker demand in some overseas markets and as they increased overseas production to counter fears of a stronger yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Steelmakers, such as Nippon Steel Corp , were largely flat after the U.S. Senate on Tuesday blocked a bill to put quotas on foreign steel imports.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. government earlier in the week launched a new anti-dumping investigation into cold-rolled steel imports from 12 countries, including Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Hiroko Nakata, Tokyo Equities Desk +81-3 5473 3714 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6n05z9b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
