\par\par \b \uc2  = \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Accounts Stay Cautious On Tsys Amid  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonoko Setaishi \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 14:15\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Despite the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to weaken its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of the dollar, Japanese investors aren't exactly snapping up Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  A stronger dollar makes Treasurys more attractive to overseas investors by promising windfall \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains when the proceeds are repatriated into their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears determined to keep the yen weak, after stepping into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market four times in less than two weeks to support the dollar and the euro against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But skepticism over the BOJ's long-term commitment to a weaker yen is keeping most Japanese investors on the sidelines, traders and sales managers said. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone agrees that the Bank of Japan is simply buying time" for its moribund economy to get back on a solid recovery path, said one New York-based sales manager at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "The economy has barely stopped sinking but is still on a very fragile footing - and you don't leave the buds out in the wind at a time like this," he added. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen helps the Japanese economy by aiding its huge export industry. \par \par \uc2  And some Japanese investors believe the BOJ will end its vigilance and allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen before long, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Do they think this is the beginning of a long-lasting trend toward a weak yen? The answer is no," the sales manager said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, Japanese accounts are worried that the closely watched "tankan" survey of business sentiment - slated for release July 5 - will prove strong and boost the yen back up. \par \par \uc2  "There are still worries that the tankan will lift the yen quite a bit," said Yasushi Muramatsu, who overseas fixed-income trading at Daiwa Securities America Inc. in New York. "Right now, the mood is still very cautious." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    An Opportunity To Bail Out?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders also cite worries - shared by local and overseas players alike\par - that the Federal Reserve may tighten its monetary policy further after\par the widely-expected one-quarter percentage point interest-rate increase\par at its policy-setting meeting on June 29 and 30.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "(Japanese) short-term players are only worried about where the yields\par will be at the end of the quarter, whereas longer-term investors are\par more concerned with the Fed than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," the sales manager\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Treasurys staged a modest advance overnight Monday, when Japanese life\par insurers and trust banks were said to have bought five-year and 10-year\par maturities to take advantage of the dollar's climb following the latest\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But a sharp sell-off during the U.S. trading - led by hedge funds,\par mortgage-related accounts and underwriters of corporate and agency deals\par - more than offset those gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "An incident like this will make (Japanese investors) even more\par reluctant to buy Treasurys," Muramatsu said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Furthermore, recent speculation that the Japanese government is\par supporting the euro in an effort to allow its investors to bail out of\par loss-making euro-denominated assets may also hold true with their U.S.\par investments, said another New York-based sales manager.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are a lot of players who bought (U.S. assets) over the years at\par Y125 and even Y140," said the sales manager. "As far as I'm concerned,\par this is the government's way of telling investors to bail out while the\par yen's relatively weak," and their unrealized losses on U.S. investments\par relatively small, he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Those investors were hit by both the yen's appreciation and the\par falling yields on Treasurys over the past decades, he noted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro's prolonged decline against the yen this year fueled rumors\par that Japanese life insurers were selling off euro zone bonds to cut\par losses.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ's euro-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday was widely believed to have\par been an attempt to rescue those investors. Before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some\par major life insurance firms denied that they were making major change in\par their global fixed-income portfolio.\par    -Sonoko Setaishi; 201-938-4148; sonoko.setaishi@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (This story was originally publised Monday)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  06-22-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0815EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv6m013n6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
