\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr ends US mixed amid dearth of new trading facts. \b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 23:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 22 (Reuters) - The dollar ended mixed on Tuesday, ending flat against the yen while gaining some ground on the euro as investors appeared happy to let the market drift against a backdrop of few new facts. \par \par \uc2  "It was a difficult day because there was not much news, there was very little going on and probably the most important thing is that the overall picture has not changed very much," Barclays Capital senior economist Henry Willmore said. \par \par \uc2  Hours after advancing in the wake of another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and awkward comments from a key European leader, the dollar's momentum sputtered. \par \par \uc2  At the close dollar/yen stood at 121.80/90, unchanged from the open while euro/dollar slipped to $1.0316/26 from $1.0317/19. Euro/yen, which had been the target of recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stood at 125.65, up modestly from 125.54 in early U.S. trading. \par \par \uc2  Traders were quick to explain the reasons behind the day's general lack of activity. \par \par \uc2  "There is no real data to speak of, everyone expects the Fed to raise interest rates next week and people are still on edge about the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so why push things either way?" one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Even though the yen probed modestly higher, still supported by optimism that Japan's sluggish economy is finally on the road to full recovery, the move was not significant enough to draw the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market again. \par \par \uc2  "The absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicates some official satisfaction with current levels," Mellon Bank strategist Marc Chandler said, as dollar/yen hung comfortably above 121 yen. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile Japan's top financial diplomat tried to quell the idea that Tokyo had identified a limit for dollar gains. Eisuke Sakakibara told a Japanese newspaper recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were aimed at "eliminating bullish yen sentiment from the market." \par \par \uc2  Thomson Financial Markets technical analyst Tom O'Malley said that technicals remain bullish on dollar/yen and as long as the pair holds above 120.90, there is a good chance for a push toward the 123 level or higher. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile in Europe, a member of Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board and the president of France's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  both helped the euro inch briefly higher as they expressed confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank member Edgar Meister said Germany's economy could grow more forcefully in the second half of the year, a development which would help lift the euro. \par \par \uc2  Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet said the euro has significant potential to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  Traders were only mildly interested however, recalling instead earlier statistics which showed that French industrial production had slipped 0.6 percent in April while German industrial production for April was revised lower to 0.7 percent from 1.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  But here too the dollar seemed to be stuck in a range, unable to gain the momentum needed to make another run at life time lows near $1.0258. Technicians said as long as $1.0290 holds, the dollar's upside will be limited. \par \par \uc2  Overall the market still expects the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike interest rates next week, something which should held the dollar in the longer term, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the British pound rose to $1.5907/17 from $1.5886/91 as Britain's Trade and Industry Secretary Stephen Byers insisted there is no question that Britain is drifting into the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that Britain is strong enough to have its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 1.5447/57 Swiss francs from 1.5460/65 francs and eased against the Canadian dollar, falling to C$1.4688/98 from C$1.4707/12. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar closed at $0.6540/45, up from $0.6503/13. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6m0605x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
