\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar softer in Tokyo, lonely without BOJ help. \b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1999\par 01:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 23 (Reuters) - The dollar started softer against the yen in Tokyo on Wednesday after a downbeat offshore session showed the market's natural bias was to sell when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders said the dollar's failure to test resistance at 122.55 yen suggested the BOJ had not succeeded in turning market sentiment and only fear of more yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was preventing a test of 121.45/50. \par \par \uc2  Some traders suspected the yen would pull back a little in the morning as the market squared up in case the BOJ did step in or Japanese officials said something noteworthy, but expected the pressure to resume as the day progressed. \par \par \uc2  By 2300 GMT the dollar was huddled at 121.84/87 yen from 121.80/90 in New York and 121.90/92 late here on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the 122.55 yen barricade was manned by Japanese exporters, U.S. funds and options players, while bids around 121.45/50 were thought to be semi-official in nature, being largely from Japanese public funds. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also underpinned by constant selling of euro/yen as distressed Japanese institutions hedged their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses on euro holdings. This kept the euro down at 125.84/93 yen, a long way from the 127.35 high reached after Friday's and Monday's BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders half suspect the BOJ's real aim is to give these institutions better levels to bail out at and there is much speculation it will not stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the euro tops 130.00 yen. With the euro around $1.0400, that implies a dollar/yen rate above 125.00. \par \par \uc2  But there are many theories on why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sanctioning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at such high dlr/yen levels. \par \par \uc2  One increasing favourite is that the authorities know a strong domestic economic figure is coming - the tankan report on corporate sentiment due on July 5 is the obvious contender - and are trying to build a cushion for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  If so, it suggests the authorities will be keen to keep the dollar up at these levels or higher for another two weeks, quite a task when the rest of world is looking to buy yen. The level of offshore interest in buying yen is seen right across Asia where stock markets are booming and signs of economic recovery seem to come in every piece of data. \par \par \uc2  "Naturally fund managers want to invest in Japan too, it is by far the biggest market in the region," said one trader. "The MOF's problem is that it wants the investment, but without a corresponding rise in the yen, and those aims seem mutually exclusive." \par \par \uc2  ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6m0605e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
